WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.500
&gt;&gt; Welcome to the Database and

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:07.905
the Data Analytics session.

00:00:07.906 --> 00:00:09.630
This is of course
a very important area,

00:00:09.631 --> 00:00:11.455
but not an entirely new area.

00:00:11.456 --> 00:00:13.140
But today, we have
three excellent

00:00:13.141 --> 00:00:15.030
speakers and they're
going to talk

00:00:15.031 --> 00:00:17.555
about some of
the very new directions

00:00:17.556 --> 00:00:19.195
that the field is taking.

00:00:19.196 --> 00:00:22.620
So, one common trait among
them is the intersection of

00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:24.270
this field with some

00:00:24.271 --> 00:00:26.005
of the techniques in
Machine Learning.

00:00:26.006 --> 00:00:27.930
The first two talks
that we are going to

00:00:27.931 --> 00:00:30.390
hear are going to be around

00:00:30.391 --> 00:00:34.680
leveraging some of these
Deep Analytic techniques for

00:00:34.681 --> 00:00:39.280
some of the classical database
operations and challenges.

00:00:39.281 --> 00:00:42.200
The last talk is going
to focus on saying

00:00:42.201 --> 00:00:45.425
how can Data Analytic platforms
who are, of course,

00:00:45.426 --> 00:00:47.450
for a long time and
traditionally have been

00:00:47.451 --> 00:00:50.660
used to understand
what the data is,

00:00:50.661 --> 00:00:52.495
what the insights in the data is,

00:00:52.496 --> 00:00:55.130
can more easily adapt to

00:00:55.131 --> 00:00:56.695
the challenges of doing

00:00:56.696 --> 00:00:59.305
Machine Learning on
these platforms.

00:00:59.306 --> 00:01:00.760
So, with these three talks,

00:01:00.761 --> 00:01:02.960
you're going to see
both dimensions of

00:01:02.961 --> 00:01:06.045
the work that's going on in
the field which is quite new.

00:01:06.046 --> 00:01:07.700
So with that, we're
going to start with

00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:09.780
the first speaker
who is Andy Pavlo.

00:01:09.781 --> 00:01:13.605
Professor Andy
Pavlo is in CMU and

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:15.090
he is the leader of

00:01:15.091 --> 00:01:16.110
the Database Group and the

00:01:16.111 --> 00:01:18.280
Parallel Database
Laboratory there.

00:01:18.281 --> 00:01:20.890
He's a winner of the SIGMOD

00:01:20.891 --> 00:01:23.845
2014 Jim Gray Dissertation Award,

00:01:23.846 --> 00:01:27.170
as well as the Sloan Research
Fellowship Award for

00:01:27.171 --> 00:01:31.540
2018 and ACM SIGMOD
Best Paper Award.

00:01:31.541 --> 00:01:34.080
In addition to
doing excellent and

00:01:34.081 --> 00:01:36.755
innovative work in
database systems,

00:01:36.756 --> 00:01:40.760
Andy is one of the most
entertaining speaker

00:01:40.761 --> 00:01:42.650
and he has promised
us to kick off

00:01:42.651 --> 00:01:44.390
this session on a high note.

00:01:44.391 --> 00:01:46.180
With that Andy, take it away.

00:01:46.181 --> 00:01:48.115
&gt;&gt; I promised you that I
wouldn't get you fired,

00:01:48.116 --> 00:01:49.240
that's what I promised you.

00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:50.360
&gt;&gt; That promise is also very

00:01:50.361 --> 00:01:52.340
important and my manager
is in the audience,

00:01:52.341 --> 00:01:53.710
so I have to be
particularly careful.

00:01:53.711 --> 00:01:55.895
&gt;&gt; Right, okay. All
right. Thanks, Surajit.

00:01:55.896 --> 00:01:59.540
So, I want to talk about
research we were doing at

00:01:59.541 --> 00:02:01.010
Carnegie Mellon on
the context of how to

00:02:01.011 --> 00:02:04.035
build autonomous or
self-driving database systems.

00:02:04.036 --> 00:02:05.735
So, by autonomous, I mean

00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:08.600
a database that's
able to configure,

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:10.055
tune and optimize itself

00:02:10.056 --> 00:02:13.180
automatically without
any human intervention.

00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:16.160
So, there's another talk
I could give about

00:02:16.161 --> 00:02:17.375
the Machine Learning methods and

00:02:17.376 --> 00:02:19.395
algorithms we use to
make this happen.

00:02:19.396 --> 00:02:21.320
Instead, this talk,
I want to talk about

00:02:21.321 --> 00:02:23.060
the things you need
to have when you're

00:02:23.061 --> 00:02:24.740
building your database system in

00:02:24.741 --> 00:02:26.690
order to make it amenable to

00:02:26.691 --> 00:02:28.185
being tuned by

00:02:28.186 --> 00:02:30.700
an autonomous component
or planning framework.

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:33.370
So, a way to think about
this, for this talk is,

00:02:33.371 --> 00:02:35.914
these are the engineering
problems we have to overcome

00:02:35.915 --> 00:02:37.540
in order to have the
Machine Learning

00:02:37.541 --> 00:02:38.705
to stop being able to control us.

00:02:38.706 --> 00:02:39.470
It doesn't matter how great

00:02:39.471 --> 00:02:40.790
your Machine
Learning algorithms are,

00:02:40.791 --> 00:02:42.225
how much training data you have,

00:02:42.226 --> 00:02:45.005
if you actually cannot expose
the right interfaces or

00:02:45.006 --> 00:02:47.180
API to those planning
frameworks and

00:02:47.181 --> 00:02:49.555
then take the actions that
they suggest and apply them,

00:02:49.556 --> 00:02:51.060
then all of it is for nothing.

00:02:51.061 --> 00:02:53.830
So, today's talk, I'm going
to break it into three parts.

00:02:53.831 --> 00:02:54.860
So first, let's talk about

00:02:54.861 --> 00:02:57.140
some background of what
work's been done in

00:02:57.141 --> 00:02:59.330
the context of
autonomous databases

00:02:59.331 --> 00:03:00.990
for the last 40 years.

00:03:00.991 --> 00:03:02.060
Then, we'll talk about more of

00:03:02.061 --> 00:03:03.370
the engineering side of things,

00:03:03.371 --> 00:03:05.640
again, how to support
Tom's operation.

00:03:05.641 --> 00:03:07.610
Then, what kind of
database talk would I

00:03:07.611 --> 00:03:10.555
give if we didn't finish off
with a rant about Oracle.

00:03:10.556 --> 00:03:15.460
So, we'll finish up
with that. So, the idea

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:17.090
of autonomous
databases are almost

00:03:17.091 --> 00:03:19.370
as old as databases themselves.

00:03:19.371 --> 00:03:21.170
The first relational databases,

00:03:21.171 --> 00:03:22.775
when they came out in the 1970s,

00:03:22.776 --> 00:03:24.905
immediately people
recognized that

00:03:24.906 --> 00:03:27.255
there was a need for these
systems to be able to adapt

00:03:27.256 --> 00:03:30.845
themselves and optimize
the performance

00:03:30.846 --> 00:03:32.780
of storage and indexing

00:03:32.781 --> 00:03:34.645
without having a human
tell it everything.

00:03:34.646 --> 00:03:36.659
So, back then, in the 1970s,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.360
this line of work was called
self-adaptive systems.

00:03:39.361 --> 00:03:41.200
It did things like
picking indexes,

00:03:41.201 --> 00:03:42.645
it's probably
the most common one.

00:03:42.646 --> 00:03:45.400
So, at a high-level,
these early self-adaptive tools

00:03:45.401 --> 00:03:47.600
worked a lot like
they work today,

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:49.310
where you have some human

00:03:49.311 --> 00:03:51.770
prepare a workload trace
of previous queries that

00:03:51.771 --> 00:03:53.930
the application has executed and

00:03:53.931 --> 00:03:56.120
then you're gonna feed them
into your tuning algorithm,

00:03:56.121 --> 00:03:57.490
but then it's going
to crunch on them,

00:03:57.491 --> 00:03:59.210
compute some internal statistics

00:03:59.211 --> 00:04:01.730
about the problem
you're trying to solve.

00:04:01.731 --> 00:04:02.990
So, if you're trying
to pick indexes,

00:04:02.991 --> 00:04:04.640
you'll figure out what columns

00:04:04.641 --> 00:04:06.570
are accessing those
often in queries.

00:04:06.571 --> 00:04:08.840
Then, you use
this internal cost model

00:04:08.841 --> 00:04:10.070
to evaluate a bunch of

00:04:10.071 --> 00:04:12.230
candidate actions or
candidate indexes

00:04:12.231 --> 00:04:13.975
and then weight them accordingly.

00:04:13.976 --> 00:04:15.665
Then the tool would spit out

00:04:15.666 --> 00:04:17.840
a recommendation to
a human who then

00:04:17.841 --> 00:04:19.610
had to make the final
decision about how

00:04:19.611 --> 00:04:21.845
and when to apply
it to the database.

00:04:21.846 --> 00:04:23.270
So, at a high level again,

00:04:23.271 --> 00:04:25.055
all of the self-adapted tools

00:04:25.056 --> 00:04:26.685
work essentially the same way.

00:04:26.686 --> 00:04:28.490
So they handle things
like index selection,

00:04:28.491 --> 00:04:31.300
data partitioning, sharding
and data placement.

00:04:31.301 --> 00:04:34.100
So, to just give you an idea
how old this problem is,

00:04:34.101 --> 00:04:36.280
there's a paper in 1976

00:04:36.281 --> 00:04:39.410
on doing self-adaptive
systems for index selection.

00:04:39.411 --> 00:04:41.105
This paper was
actually written by

00:04:41.106 --> 00:04:44.315
my advisor's advisor
who is now dead.

00:04:44.316 --> 00:04:45.830
So, this again, people
will be thinking

00:04:45.831 --> 00:04:47.755
about this for a long time.

00:04:47.756 --> 00:04:49.745
So, now, the next

00:04:49.746 --> 00:04:51.800
major trainer chapter in
autonomous systems came

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:55.565
along in the late
1990s and 2000s.

00:04:55.566 --> 00:04:57.080
This entered the era of what

00:04:57.081 --> 00:04:59.460
I'll call self-tuning databases.

00:04:59.461 --> 00:05:00.980
Again, at a high, all these tools

00:05:00.981 --> 00:05:02.060
are essentially doing
the same thing.

00:05:02.061 --> 00:05:03.560
You take some workload
trace, crunch on

00:05:03.561 --> 00:05:05.810
them and they spit
out recommendations.

00:05:05.811 --> 00:05:08.540
So, all the major
vendors at this time

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:10.310
had their own proprietary tools

00:05:10.311 --> 00:05:12.025
to solve these types of problems.

00:05:12.026 --> 00:05:14.250
I'm not just saying this because
we're here at Microsoft,

00:05:14.251 --> 00:05:15.795
but it's my opinion that

00:05:15.796 --> 00:05:19.005
the AutoAdmin project by
Microsoft was at the vanguard,

00:05:19.006 --> 00:05:20.080
really at the forefront of this.

00:05:20.081 --> 00:05:20.840
There's a lot of

00:05:20.841 --> 00:05:22.550
great groundbreaking work
that we're actually

00:05:22.551 --> 00:05:25.945
building on today that
came out of this project.

00:05:25.946 --> 00:05:30.515
The seminal paper would be
this 2007 paper from Surajit.

00:05:30.516 --> 00:05:32.540
It talks about the decades worth

00:05:32.541 --> 00:05:34.220
of work they did on this project.

00:05:34.221 --> 00:05:36.070
So, the other thing we

00:05:36.071 --> 00:05:37.550
saw in the self-tuning
systems around

00:05:37.551 --> 00:05:41.325
this time was the need to
do knob configuration.

00:05:41.326 --> 00:05:43.135
So, knob would be a

00:05:43.136 --> 00:05:45.110
configuration parameter
you could set in

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:46.700
the system to control

00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:48.895
the runtime behavior
of the database,

00:05:48.896 --> 00:05:51.855
how much memory do you
use for your buffer pool.

00:05:51.856 --> 00:05:54.470
So the reason why there was now

00:05:54.471 --> 00:05:57.110
a need for automatic tools
to be able to configure

00:05:57.111 --> 00:05:58.730
these things is
because the number

00:05:58.731 --> 00:05:59.990
of knobs that these
systems actually

00:05:59.991 --> 00:06:01.820
supported kept increasing and

00:06:01.821 --> 00:06:03.190
things were getting more complex.

00:06:03.191 --> 00:06:05.030
To give you an idea
how bad it is,

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:06.470
one of my students went and

00:06:06.471 --> 00:06:09.560
took two major open-source
databases MySQL and

00:06:09.561 --> 00:06:12.125
Postgres and went
back 15 years and

00:06:12.126 --> 00:06:13.610
looked at all the
different releases and

00:06:13.611 --> 00:06:15.290
counted the number of
knobs that they had.

00:06:15.291 --> 00:06:17.175
In over a 15-year period,

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:18.770
Postgres increased
the number of knobs by

00:06:18.771 --> 00:06:20.370
five x and MySQL increased

00:06:20.371 --> 00:06:22.195
the number of knobs
they had by seven x.

00:06:22.196 --> 00:06:24.050
So this point, this is well

00:06:24.051 --> 00:06:25.840
beyond what any human
can reason about.

00:06:25.841 --> 00:06:26.900
So, there's automated tools

00:06:26.901 --> 00:06:28.855
that are needed to
make this happen.

00:06:28.856 --> 00:06:31.620
So, now we see in
the 2010s we all

00:06:31.621 --> 00:06:32.680
recall when we entered the era of

00:06:32.681 --> 00:06:34.135
Cloud-managed databases.

00:06:34.136 --> 00:06:36.610
This is exactly what we
just saw in the keynote,

00:06:36.611 --> 00:06:38.780
where it was not so
much about how to tune

00:06:38.781 --> 00:06:42.530
individual databases was now
at a service provider level,

00:06:42.531 --> 00:06:44.270
how to essentially do
a bin packing problem.

00:06:44.271 --> 00:06:46.625
How to place the tenants

00:06:46.626 --> 00:06:48.410
on the various
hardware that you have

00:06:48.411 --> 00:06:50.360
available in order to maximize

00:06:50.361 --> 00:06:52.780
the performance by minimizing
your hardware costs,

00:06:52.781 --> 00:06:55.110
and you may need to actually
migrate things over time.

00:06:55.111 --> 00:06:56.350
So, this is again,

00:06:56.351 --> 00:06:58.100
this is doing
autonomous databases,

00:06:58.101 --> 00:06:59.540
but at the service
provider level or

00:06:59.541 --> 00:07:01.130
the operative level where you're

00:07:01.131 --> 00:07:02.870
not actually tuning
individual databases,

00:07:02.871 --> 00:07:04.565
you're just looking at
thousands and thousands of

00:07:04.566 --> 00:07:05.725
tenants and trying to

00:07:05.726 --> 00:07:07.995
decide what's the best way
to lay them out.

00:07:07.996 --> 00:07:11.620
So, now, given I just
spent this time,

00:07:11.621 --> 00:07:13.080
the beginning, talking
about the last 40 years

00:07:13.081 --> 00:07:14.729
of autonomous databases,

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:17.870
why is all this work that
people have done before,

00:07:17.871 --> 00:07:19.745
why is this insufficient for

00:07:19.746 --> 00:07:22.315
achieving a fully
autonomous system?

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:24.980
I'll say there's three
reasons why this is

00:07:24.981 --> 00:07:27.825
not entirely what we need.

00:07:27.826 --> 00:07:29.570
So, the first is that a lot of

00:07:29.571 --> 00:07:31.520
these tools still
require a human to make

00:07:31.521 --> 00:07:33.230
a final judgment about

00:07:33.231 --> 00:07:35.210
the recommendations
or suggestions

00:07:35.211 --> 00:07:36.840
from these tuning tools.

00:07:36.841 --> 00:07:38.775
So, a human had to
look and say well,

00:07:38.776 --> 00:07:40.480
"It's asked me to
build this index.

00:07:40.481 --> 00:07:42.645
Is that actually the right
thing for me to do?"

00:07:42.646 --> 00:07:44.650
Then the human actually
had to then decide

00:07:44.651 --> 00:07:45.950
when it was the right time to

00:07:45.951 --> 00:07:49.505
deploy that suggestion and
how should they deploy it.

00:07:49.506 --> 00:07:51.470
So they had to know
that three AM on

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:54.260
a Sunday is when I have
the lowest amount of demand,

00:07:54.261 --> 00:07:57.770
so that's when I go ahead
and start optimizing things.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.170
The next problem is
that these are all

00:08:00.171 --> 00:08:03.785
reactionary measures, a lot
of them are reactionary,

00:08:03.786 --> 00:08:06.140
meaning they look at
the workload trace in the past,

00:08:06.141 --> 00:08:08.005
identify problems and try to

00:08:08.006 --> 00:08:11.030
cough suggestions and
avoid them in the future.

00:08:11.031 --> 00:08:12.470
They're not looking down the road

00:08:12.471 --> 00:08:14.100
looking at various trends
and saying, "Well,

00:08:14.101 --> 00:08:15.500
this is what my workload
is going to look

00:08:15.501 --> 00:08:17.839
like a week from now
or a month from now",

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:19.645
and prepare the
system accordingly.

00:08:19.646 --> 00:08:21.360
This is essentially what
humans are doing now.

00:08:21.361 --> 00:08:23.540
They do capacity planning
based on what

00:08:23.541 --> 00:08:24.830
they think that the workload is

00:08:24.831 --> 00:08:26.650
going to look like in the future.

00:08:26.651 --> 00:08:28.950
The last problem is
that they're not,

00:08:28.951 --> 00:08:30.080
not be able to transfer

00:08:30.081 --> 00:08:31.490
any of the knowledge
that they learned from

00:08:31.491 --> 00:08:32.990
tuning one particular database

00:08:32.991 --> 00:08:34.805
and apply it to another database.

00:08:34.806 --> 00:08:37.280
So what that means is that,
say I run my tuning tool or

00:08:37.281 --> 00:08:39.920
my single database instance
and then I come

00:08:39.921 --> 00:08:41.780
along with another application

00:08:41.781 --> 00:08:43.320
I want to tune this database for,

00:08:43.321 --> 00:08:44.670
unless that application has

00:08:44.671 --> 00:08:48.570
exactly the same workload
on the exact same hardware,

00:08:48.571 --> 00:08:50.360
you can't reuse anything you

00:08:50.361 --> 00:08:52.730
learn from the first
time you ran it.

00:08:52.731 --> 00:08:55.160
So, these are the
reasons why I think

00:08:55.161 --> 00:08:56.945
all the existing work is not

00:08:56.946 --> 00:08:59.390
enough in order to have
a fully autonomous system.

00:08:59.391 --> 00:09:01.580
So, now you may be
asking why am I so

00:09:01.581 --> 00:09:04.365
confident that it's going
to be different this time.

00:09:04.366 --> 00:09:06.405
So, I would say
that the reason why

00:09:06.406 --> 00:09:08.660
I'm optimistic about
our ability to have

00:09:08.661 --> 00:09:10.670
a fully autonomous
system is essentially

00:09:10.671 --> 00:09:13.160
the same story why Machine
Learning is hot now.

00:09:13.161 --> 00:09:15.650
We have the storage capacity
actually store a lot of

00:09:15.651 --> 00:09:17.300
training data and then we have

00:09:17.301 --> 00:09:19.805
better frameworks like
Torch and TensorFlow,

00:09:19.806 --> 00:09:22.250
even a crunch on it
and then we have

00:09:22.251 --> 00:09:25.320
hardware accelerators like
GPUs and CPUs available to us,

00:09:25.321 --> 00:09:27.290
to be able to take large amounts
of this training data

00:09:27.291 --> 00:09:29.895
and derive the
models that we need.

00:09:29.896 --> 00:09:32.675
So, this is why I'm
bullish on this area,

00:09:32.676 --> 00:09:34.610
and I think that there's a lot
of interesting techniques

00:09:34.611 --> 00:09:36.740
from Machine Learning and
the AI community that we

00:09:36.741 --> 00:09:38.960
can apply for our databases
to essentially complete

00:09:38.961 --> 00:09:42.060
the circle from what people
have done in the past.

00:09:42.061 --> 00:09:43.990
So, to give you an idea

00:09:43.991 --> 00:09:46.570
what some of these
projects look like,

00:09:46.571 --> 00:09:47.810
I want to talk about
two things we've been

00:09:47.811 --> 00:09:50.205
building at Carnegie Mellon
for the last couple of years.

00:09:50.206 --> 00:09:52.100
The first is this thing
called OtterTune,

00:09:52.101 --> 00:09:55.140
which is a knob configuration
tuning service.

00:09:55.141 --> 00:09:56.485
This is designed to work on

00:09:56.486 --> 00:09:59.830
existing systems like SQL
Server or MySQL Postgres,

00:09:59.831 --> 00:10:01.400
and treating them as a black box,

00:10:01.401 --> 00:10:03.520
and trying to figure out
what information can we

00:10:03.521 --> 00:10:06.090
derive from them to help
our tuner knobs better.

00:10:06.091 --> 00:10:08.475
Then we have another database
has been called Peloton,

00:10:08.476 --> 00:10:10.075
we'll be burning
this from scratch,

00:10:10.076 --> 00:10:11.390
where the idea is to take

00:10:11.391 --> 00:10:13.550
a clean-slate approach
at designing

00:10:13.551 --> 00:10:15.700
a database system
in order to make it

00:10:15.701 --> 00:10:19.155
be managed by
an autonomous system.

00:10:19.156 --> 00:10:21.895
So, I'll briefly go over
each of these one by one.

00:10:21.896 --> 00:10:24.240
So, as I said, OtterTune is

00:10:24.241 --> 00:10:26.380
a database knob
Tuning-as-a-Service.

00:10:26.381 --> 00:10:27.755
The idea is, you come along

00:10:27.756 --> 00:10:29.130
with your database installation,

00:10:29.131 --> 00:10:30.220
you connect it to a service,

00:10:30.221 --> 00:10:31.530
you upload some metrics,

00:10:31.531 --> 00:10:32.960
we crunch on them, and then spit

00:10:32.961 --> 00:10:34.540
back a knob recommendation,

00:10:34.541 --> 00:10:36.300
and then we apply that observe

00:10:36.301 --> 00:10:37.750
your changes and see
whether that helps.

00:10:37.751 --> 00:10:38.945
In this feedback loop,

00:10:38.946 --> 00:10:40.310
you get better and better.

00:10:40.311 --> 00:10:42.265
The key difference
about OtterTune

00:10:42.266 --> 00:10:43.405
than all the previous work,

00:10:43.406 --> 00:10:45.930
is that we can actually
re-use the information,

00:10:45.931 --> 00:10:47.645
or the data we've collected from

00:10:47.646 --> 00:10:49.210
previous tuning sessions to

00:10:49.211 --> 00:10:51.315
help speed up tuning
newer sessions.

00:10:51.316 --> 00:10:52.850
So, that means if
you come along with

00:10:52.851 --> 00:10:54.850
your application that we've
never seen it before,

00:10:54.851 --> 00:10:56.260
but we collect some metrics and

00:10:56.261 --> 00:10:57.530
we look to see how
it actually looks

00:10:57.531 --> 00:10:58.660
very similar to this other

00:10:58.661 --> 00:11:00.120
application we tuned in the past,

00:11:00.121 --> 00:11:01.865
since we knew how
to tune that one,

00:11:01.866 --> 00:11:03.980
now we know how to tune yours.

00:11:03.981 --> 00:11:08.195
So, just as a quick show
what performance we can get,

00:11:08.196 --> 00:11:09.590
so we did a simple
experiment where

00:11:09.591 --> 00:11:11.265
we took MySQL and Postgres,

00:11:11.266 --> 00:11:15.190
we ran the TPCC benchmark on
a small instance on Amazon.

00:11:15.191 --> 00:11:16.840
We're going to compare
what OtterTune can

00:11:16.841 --> 00:11:18.500
do against the default
configuration you

00:11:18.501 --> 00:11:19.990
get for these systems versus

00:11:19.991 --> 00:11:21.680
some open-source tuning scripts,

00:11:21.681 --> 00:11:22.815
or the configuration you get from

00:11:22.816 --> 00:11:24.660
Amazon when you run an RDS,.

00:11:24.661 --> 00:11:26.630
Then lastly, we're also
going to compare it against

00:11:26.631 --> 00:11:28.585
very expensive human DBAs

00:11:28.586 --> 00:11:31.295
tuning these two
systems manually.

00:11:31.296 --> 00:11:33.995
So, in the case of MySQL
what you see is that,

00:11:33.996 --> 00:11:35.750
the OtterTune and the DBA

00:11:35.751 --> 00:11:38.499
actually performed better than
all the other approaches.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.120
Now, the reason why the DBA
actually beats us here,

00:11:41.121 --> 00:11:44.870
is because this is the top
MySQL DBA from Facebook,

00:11:44.871 --> 00:11:47.700
and there's some knob
in MySQL that affects

00:11:47.701 --> 00:11:48.990
whether you flush every

00:11:48.991 --> 00:11:51.100
right when each
commit a transaction,

00:11:51.101 --> 00:11:53.305
or how aggressive
you flush things.

00:11:53.306 --> 00:11:55.915
At Facebook, they were
okay with turning

00:11:55.916 --> 00:11:57.040
that feature off so that it

00:11:57.041 --> 00:11:58.885
allows you get a little bit
better performance.

00:11:58.886 --> 00:12:00.340
This is actually
an important point when

00:12:00.341 --> 00:12:01.675
we talk about
the engineering side,

00:12:01.676 --> 00:12:04.120
because this actually
a human judgment

00:12:04.121 --> 00:12:05.260
has to be made about whether it's

00:12:05.261 --> 00:12:09.365
okay to reduce the durability
of your transactional data.

00:12:09.366 --> 00:12:12.655
So, we blacklisted
this knob from OtterTune.

00:12:12.656 --> 00:12:15.630
So, we purposely said it's
not allowed to tune this,

00:12:15.631 --> 00:12:19.190
and then that's why the DBA
was actually able to beat us.

00:12:19.191 --> 00:12:21.345
In the case of Postgres,

00:12:21.346 --> 00:12:22.950
we see the OtterTune
actually does

00:12:22.951 --> 00:12:24.860
better than all the
other approaches,

00:12:24.861 --> 00:12:28.265
and this is because it's able
to find the sweet spot for

00:12:28.266 --> 00:12:29.640
this particular workload and

00:12:29.641 --> 00:12:33.755
harbor configuration for
tuning the buffer pool size,

00:12:33.756 --> 00:12:35.660
that's the log file size.

00:12:35.661 --> 00:12:38.395
This again, we're up against
a very seasoned DBA,

00:12:38.396 --> 00:12:40.615
and we are able to beat them.

00:12:40.616 --> 00:12:42.050
Again, the main thing here is

00:12:42.051 --> 00:12:44.910
that OtterTune is doing as good,

00:12:44.911 --> 00:12:48.375
if not better than
very expensive human DBAs,

00:12:48.376 --> 00:12:50.480
with only training
for about an hour.

00:12:50.481 --> 00:12:53.810
Now, the next project is Peleton,

00:12:53.811 --> 00:12:54.800
and we're pitching this as

00:12:54.801 --> 00:12:56.440
the self-driving database system.

00:12:56.441 --> 00:12:57.850
Meaning that we want
this thing to be

00:12:57.851 --> 00:12:59.435
entirely autonomous.

00:12:59.436 --> 00:13:01.860
So, we're building this
from the ground up to be

00:13:01.861 --> 00:13:03.810
entirely controlled

00:13:03.811 --> 00:13:06.345
by this self-driving framework
we've been building.

00:13:06.346 --> 00:13:08.345
The idea here is
that we want to see,

00:13:08.346 --> 00:13:10.580
what advantage do we have when

00:13:10.581 --> 00:13:12.650
we have complete control
of the entire system?

00:13:12.651 --> 00:13:14.160
How it allows to do things that

00:13:14.161 --> 00:13:16.290
we cannot easily
do with OtterTune.

00:13:16.291 --> 00:13:18.400
What I'll say, is that we
actually considered maybe

00:13:18.401 --> 00:13:21.080
using MySQL Postgres
initially for this project,

00:13:21.081 --> 00:13:22.710
but what we found is
that there's just way

00:13:22.711 --> 00:13:25.760
too many things that require
the system to restart,

00:13:25.761 --> 00:13:27.165
have other problems
that make it not

00:13:27.166 --> 00:13:29.825
amenable for
self-driving operation.

00:13:29.826 --> 00:13:31.360
So, we decided to bite the bullet

00:13:31.361 --> 00:13:33.205
and write a system from scratch.

00:13:33.206 --> 00:13:34.300
There's a whole other talk I can

00:13:34.301 --> 00:13:35.810
give about the trials and

00:13:35.811 --> 00:13:37.530
tribulations of building
a database system

00:13:37.531 --> 00:13:39.245
from scratch in academia,

00:13:39.246 --> 00:13:41.850
which is been quite a journey.

00:13:41.851 --> 00:13:43.900
So now, I want to talk

00:13:43.901 --> 00:13:45.790
about what I'm calling

00:13:45.791 --> 00:13:48.010
design considerations for
autonomous operation.

00:13:48.011 --> 00:13:49.865
So again, the idea here is that,

00:13:49.866 --> 00:13:52.145
how should you build
a database system

00:13:52.146 --> 00:13:54.340
in order to support itself,

00:13:54.341 --> 00:13:56.070
to have itself to be
able to be controlled

00:13:56.071 --> 00:13:57.995
by planning framework that

00:13:57.996 --> 00:13:59.990
wants to run entirely
autonomously.

00:13:59.991 --> 00:14:01.670
So, for this,

00:14:01.671 --> 00:14:03.895
the re-occurring theme that
we see over and over again,

00:14:03.896 --> 00:14:06.640
is that these
engineering decisions

00:14:06.641 --> 00:14:07.660
we can make are going to help

00:14:07.661 --> 00:14:11.690
us reduce the complexity

00:14:11.691 --> 00:14:13.280
of the solution space

00:14:13.281 --> 00:14:15.975
for our autonomous
planning components.

00:14:15.976 --> 00:14:17.930
Because, otherwise
we need a lot of

00:14:17.931 --> 00:14:20.575
training data in order
to train these models,

00:14:20.576 --> 00:14:22.355
and it'll take
a long time for them

00:14:22.356 --> 00:14:24.135
to converge to a good solution.

00:14:24.136 --> 00:14:25.680
So, there's some tricks we can do

00:14:25.681 --> 00:14:28.060
to reduce the number
of choices we have to

00:14:28.061 --> 00:14:30.840
consider and maximize the reuse

00:14:30.841 --> 00:14:32.740
of the training data that
we want to build upon,

00:14:32.741 --> 00:14:34.040
and that allows us to get

00:14:34.041 --> 00:14:35.730
better optimizations
more quickly.

00:14:35.731 --> 00:14:37.330
So, I'll first start talking

00:14:37.331 --> 00:14:39.775
about configuration
knobs and metrics,

00:14:39.776 --> 00:14:41.710
so the idea is here's
what API or data you

00:14:41.711 --> 00:14:44.275
expose to the planning framework,

00:14:44.276 --> 00:14:45.420
and then we'll talk
about how do you take

00:14:45.421 --> 00:14:47.980
the actions that
the planning framework suggests,

00:14:47.981 --> 00:14:49.900
and apply them in
an efficient manner without

00:14:49.901 --> 00:14:52.280
slowing down the rest
of the system.

00:14:52.281 --> 00:14:54.380
All right.

00:14:54.381 --> 00:14:56.410
So, the most important thing

00:14:56.411 --> 00:14:59.080
you have to have for
your configuration knobs is

00:14:59.081 --> 00:15:01.620
the ability to mark which
ones should or should not

00:15:01.621 --> 00:15:04.400
be tuned by your
autonomous components.

00:15:04.401 --> 00:15:07.080
We saw this before when we
talked about the Facebook DBA.

00:15:07.081 --> 00:15:08.545
There's some flag that

00:15:08.546 --> 00:15:11.040
Facebook allowed
their DBA's to set,

00:15:11.041 --> 00:15:12.610
that we decided that
we weren't going to

00:15:12.611 --> 00:15:14.640
set in our autonomous
components because

00:15:14.641 --> 00:15:17.070
a human had to make
a final value judgment

00:15:17.071 --> 00:15:18.970
about whether it was
okay to do that.

00:15:18.971 --> 00:15:20.580
So, some things are obvious.

00:15:20.581 --> 00:15:23.355
Like file parse, network
addresses, and port numbers,

00:15:23.356 --> 00:15:25.140
but if you don't set
these things then system

00:15:25.141 --> 00:15:27.055
doesn't boot properly at all.

00:15:27.056 --> 00:15:29.535
But things like durability
and isolation levels,

00:15:29.536 --> 00:15:31.880
these are actually judgments
that have to be made by

00:15:31.881 --> 00:15:34.160
the company organization about

00:15:34.161 --> 00:15:36.295
whether that was
an okay thing to do.

00:15:36.296 --> 00:15:38.285
Is it okay to turn off async

00:15:38.286 --> 00:15:40.020
when you commit a transaction?

00:15:40.021 --> 00:15:42.100
Are you okay with losing the last

00:15:42.101 --> 00:15:44.315
10 to 20 milliseconds
worth of transaction data?

00:15:44.316 --> 00:15:45.560
Some places, that's okay,

00:15:45.561 --> 00:15:46.985
some places that's not okay.

00:15:46.986 --> 00:15:48.530
But the machine learning

00:15:48.531 --> 00:15:50.235
algorithms aren't
going to do that.

00:15:50.236 --> 00:15:52.940
There's some other more nuance
things like harbor usage,

00:15:52.941 --> 00:15:54.545
like how aggressive
you want to be

00:15:54.546 --> 00:15:56.885
doing compaction if
you have an LSM.

00:15:56.886 --> 00:15:58.710
Because this will cause you
to wear down the device

00:15:58.711 --> 00:16:00.885
more quickly and have
to buy new hardware.

00:16:00.886 --> 00:16:02.580
Recovery time is another one,

00:16:02.581 --> 00:16:05.390
like how long are you
willing to have the system

00:16:05.391 --> 00:16:08.545
to take to recover
if there is a crash?

00:16:08.546 --> 00:16:11.260
We can't control
these things or we

00:16:11.261 --> 00:16:12.640
can't know these things in

00:16:12.641 --> 00:16:14.645
our Machine Learning
model across models.

00:16:14.646 --> 00:16:16.220
So, we have to have a human to be

00:16:16.221 --> 00:16:17.850
able to tell us and therefore,

00:16:17.851 --> 00:16:19.980
we need to blacklist him.

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:23.070
The next is one is
that we need now

00:16:23.071 --> 00:16:25.710
hints about how we should
actually tune these knobs.

00:16:25.711 --> 00:16:28.300
Again, this is necessary
again to reduce

00:16:28.301 --> 00:16:29.660
the search space or

00:16:29.661 --> 00:16:31.575
solution space of the actions

00:16:31.576 --> 00:16:33.280
that we could apply
in our system.

00:16:33.281 --> 00:16:34.805
So, the most obvious one, is

00:16:34.806 --> 00:16:37.980
our min-max ranges
for a parameter.

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:40.180
How much memory should I be able

00:16:40.181 --> 00:16:42.155
to allocate to a buffer pool?

00:16:42.156 --> 00:16:44.235
There's other things we
see that any time you have

00:16:44.236 --> 00:16:47.350
a knob that you think
can't be controlled,

00:16:47.351 --> 00:16:48.610
meaning turn it on and off,

00:16:48.611 --> 00:16:50.410
you should always
separate them from

00:16:50.411 --> 00:16:52.585
the actual knob that
controls its behavior.

00:16:52.586 --> 00:16:55.290
So, sometimes we see
knobs where you can rate

00:16:55.291 --> 00:16:57.020
them how fast it's going write

00:16:57.021 --> 00:16:59.060
data at the desk in
terms of kilobytes.

00:16:59.061 --> 00:17:01.170
But a lot of times, you'll
use a special value like

00:17:01.171 --> 00:17:03.845
negative one or zero to
disable that feature.

00:17:03.846 --> 00:17:06.320
The problem is, the
Machine Learning algorithm

00:17:06.321 --> 00:17:07.400
will find that setting,

00:17:07.401 --> 00:17:09.370
the amount of data
you're reading out

00:17:09.371 --> 00:17:12.010
the disc to zero kilobytes
makes it go faster.

00:17:12.011 --> 00:17:13.860
Of course, that now that
means you're not writing

00:17:13.861 --> 00:17:15.770
data to disk, and you're
going to lose data.

00:17:15.771 --> 00:17:18.090
So, if you just have a separate
boolean flag to control

00:17:18.091 --> 00:17:20.390
it separately from
the actual parameter,

00:17:20.391 --> 00:17:22.000
that makes things easier.

00:17:22.001 --> 00:17:25.400
Another more nuanced one
is the ability to

00:17:25.401 --> 00:17:28.845
control how you actually
modify these values are knobs.

00:17:28.846 --> 00:17:31.025
So, in most cases,

00:17:31.026 --> 00:17:32.700
in those systems we've seen,

00:17:32.701 --> 00:17:36.075
you can set absolute values
for a certain parameter.

00:17:36.076 --> 00:17:36.965
Like how much memory you want to

00:17:36.966 --> 00:17:38.165
use for your buffer pool,

00:17:38.166 --> 00:17:41.030
you can set it from 0 to 32.

00:17:41.031 --> 00:17:44.855
We think the better way
to do this is just have

00:17:44.856 --> 00:17:46.910
increments that
are based on delta

00:17:46.911 --> 00:17:49.070
that go up and down
for these parameters,

00:17:49.071 --> 00:17:50.090
and that cuts down the number of

00:17:50.091 --> 00:17:51.460
actions you have to consider.

00:17:51.461 --> 00:17:52.920
But now the problem is,

00:17:52.921 --> 00:17:57.730
if you have a large range
of values you could choose,

00:17:57.731 --> 00:18:00.785
then incrementing
by a fixed amount

00:18:00.786 --> 00:18:03.885
throughout that entire range
causes problems.

00:18:03.886 --> 00:18:06.560
So, what you really want is
non-uniform deltas like this.

00:18:06.561 --> 00:18:07.700
So, say if I want to
set the amount of

00:18:07.701 --> 00:18:09.155
memory of music in
my buffer pool,

00:18:09.156 --> 00:18:11.450
if I'm between one kilobyte
and one megabyte,

00:18:11.451 --> 00:18:14.200
maybe just increment by
100 kilobytes at a time.

00:18:14.201 --> 00:18:16.605
But if I'm up that
one terabyte of DRAM,

00:18:16.606 --> 00:18:18.310
I don't want to increment
by one kilobytes,

00:18:18.311 --> 00:18:20.030
because it's not going
to get big difference

00:18:20.031 --> 00:18:21.935
from one setting to the next.

00:18:21.936 --> 00:18:25.300
So, you can expose the deltas
to the tuning algorithms,

00:18:25.301 --> 00:18:26.620
and say only increment by

00:18:26.621 --> 00:18:29.490
these values when you're
within these ranges.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.070
Now, the next thing we're
going to talk about is,

00:18:32.071 --> 00:18:33.320
how do you expose metrics or

00:18:33.321 --> 00:18:34.930
information to
the outside system,

00:18:34.931 --> 00:18:37.370
and we are going to use these
to build our cost models,

00:18:37.371 --> 00:18:40.500
and do reward calculations for
our actions we can choose.

00:18:40.501 --> 00:18:42.040
So, the most important
thing we find,

00:18:42.041 --> 00:18:44.340
is that anytime your database
system supports

00:18:44.341 --> 00:18:47.170
the ability to tune
individual sub-components,

00:18:47.171 --> 00:18:48.929
you are going to
make sure you expose

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:50.910
individual metrics for
the sub-components.

00:18:50.911 --> 00:18:53.390
So, an example
would be in my DB2,

00:18:53.391 --> 00:18:55.620
you can set the number of
buffer pools you want,

00:18:55.621 --> 00:18:57.655
and for each buffer pool you
can tune them separately.

00:18:57.656 --> 00:18:59.780
You need to make sure that
for each buffer pool,

00:18:59.781 --> 00:19:03.515
all of the stats you care
about are exposed to you,

00:19:03.516 --> 00:19:05.450
and not so aggregated together.

00:19:05.451 --> 00:19:06.920
The biggest offender we find is

00:19:06.921 --> 00:19:08.475
actually for this, is RocksDB.

00:19:08.476 --> 00:19:10.770
So, in RocksDB, you can set,

00:19:10.771 --> 00:19:13.495
for each table you can have
multiple column families.

00:19:13.496 --> 00:19:16.725
You tune each column
family individually.

00:19:16.726 --> 00:19:18.855
So, they do expose

00:19:18.856 --> 00:19:21.990
some metrics about each
column family, but the problem,

00:19:21.991 --> 00:19:24.320
is the most important thing
you need to determine whether

00:19:24.321 --> 00:19:26.000
your performances
get better or worse

00:19:26.001 --> 00:19:28.020
are the number of reads and
writes per column family.

00:19:28.021 --> 00:19:32.305
But they're not included in
the subcomponent metrics.

00:19:32.306 --> 00:19:33.970
Instead, you can only get this

00:19:33.971 --> 00:19:35.735
from a global metric table,

00:19:35.736 --> 00:19:38.025
where it's been
combined together.

00:19:38.026 --> 00:19:40.230
So, now you need a lot
of training data

00:19:40.231 --> 00:19:42.250
in order to determine
whether the changes

00:19:42.251 --> 00:19:44.790
you're making to
one particular column family

00:19:44.791 --> 00:19:46.695
is benefiting or hurting you,

00:19:46.696 --> 00:19:48.590
because you have to
then extract that

00:19:48.591 --> 00:19:52.205
from the aggregated metric.

00:19:52.206 --> 00:19:54.330
So, now we are going to talk
about how do you actually

00:19:54.331 --> 00:19:55.435
take the actions that

00:19:55.436 --> 00:19:58.070
the album select and
apply them efficiently.

00:19:58.071 --> 00:19:59.850
So the most important
thing I would say,

00:19:59.851 --> 00:20:01.280
and part of the reason why with

00:20:01.281 --> 00:20:03.550
Peloton we decided to build
a system from scratch,

00:20:03.551 --> 00:20:07.320
is that we find that
many cases changing

00:20:07.321 --> 00:20:09.340
a knob or applying
an action requires you

00:20:09.341 --> 00:20:11.650
to restart the system in order
for that to take effect.

00:20:11.651 --> 00:20:13.230
Now the commercial
systems are much

00:20:13.231 --> 00:20:15.065
better than this than
the open-source guys,

00:20:15.066 --> 00:20:18.140
but even the SQL
server Oracle and DB2,

00:20:18.141 --> 00:20:19.370
there's enough knobs in there

00:20:19.371 --> 00:20:21.025
that require you to
have to restart.

00:20:21.026 --> 00:20:23.110
The reason why that's
problematic is because now

00:20:23.111 --> 00:20:25.130
you need to take
into consideration,

00:20:25.131 --> 00:20:26.590
in your cost models,

00:20:26.591 --> 00:20:29.155
the time it takes to
restart the system.

00:20:29.156 --> 00:20:30.945
Or you have to ask a human

00:20:30.946 --> 00:20:33.265
whether it's okay to restart
the system right now.

00:20:33.266 --> 00:20:34.610
So, we think that there should be

00:20:34.611 --> 00:20:35.760
never any knob that will

00:20:35.761 --> 00:20:36.925
require you to have
to restart and this

00:20:36.926 --> 00:20:38.440
includes scaling up the hardware,

00:20:38.441 --> 00:20:40.180
as well as applying changes that

00:20:40.181 --> 00:20:42.945
modify how it actually
uses the hardware.

00:20:42.946 --> 00:20:45.330
Another very important thing is

00:20:45.331 --> 00:20:47.490
that you want to have
the ability to have

00:20:47.491 --> 00:20:52.140
your replicas diverge and have
different configurations,

00:20:52.141 --> 00:20:53.940
in order to get

00:20:53.941 --> 00:20:55.240
more training data about

00:20:55.241 --> 00:20:56.855
the different configurations
you could have.

00:20:56.856 --> 00:20:59.870
Alright, so a very common setup
and databases is that for

00:20:59.871 --> 00:21:01.350
high availability
you have a master

00:21:01.351 --> 00:21:03.175
and some replicas, right?

00:21:03.176 --> 00:21:04.830
And anytime the master
goes down then you

00:21:04.831 --> 00:21:06.920
elect one of the replicas
to become the new master.

00:21:06.921 --> 00:21:08.900
So, normally you would
have these things

00:21:08.901 --> 00:21:10.660
have the exact same
configuration because

00:21:10.661 --> 00:21:12.230
anytime to master
goes down you want

00:21:12.231 --> 00:21:14.895
the replica to be able
to come up right away.

00:21:14.896 --> 00:21:17.610
But instead what you can do
to get more training data,

00:21:17.611 --> 00:21:18.820
you could have the master had

00:21:18.821 --> 00:21:21.400
the best configuration
you ever seen,

00:21:21.401 --> 00:21:22.640
but in your replicas you're

00:21:22.641 --> 00:21:24.560
trying out other
configurations see

00:21:24.561 --> 00:21:25.855
whether they're actually better

00:21:25.856 --> 00:21:27.675
than what's on the
master right now.

00:21:27.676 --> 00:21:29.420
So in this case here,
the replicas could be

00:21:29.421 --> 00:21:31.225
trying out different
index selections,

00:21:31.226 --> 00:21:32.460
and seeing whether
they're actually

00:21:32.461 --> 00:21:33.830
improving performance.

00:21:33.831 --> 00:21:35.350
Let's say for this top guy here,

00:21:35.351 --> 00:21:37.550
this is actually doing
better than we can recognize

00:21:37.551 --> 00:21:39.945
that it's okay for us to
deploy this on the master.

00:21:39.946 --> 00:21:41.410
Right? The reason why
we want to do this in

00:21:41.411 --> 00:21:42.890
the replica is we
don't want to slow

00:21:42.891 --> 00:21:45.260
down the master node

00:21:45.261 --> 00:21:47.880
because we need to meet
our SLAs and SLOs.

00:21:47.881 --> 00:21:49.530
So now the problem is though,

00:21:49.531 --> 00:21:50.900
let's say that this bottom guy

00:21:50.901 --> 00:21:53.040
here we're trying out
a configuration that

00:21:53.041 --> 00:21:55.910
actually is bad and
therefore we're

00:21:55.911 --> 00:21:59.430
falling behind the workload
that's on the master.

00:21:59.431 --> 00:22:01.000
Right? So, we recognize

00:22:01.001 --> 00:22:03.060
now we are too far behind
we want to go ahead

00:22:03.061 --> 00:22:05.580
and kill that configuration and

00:22:05.581 --> 00:22:06.850
restart the system because we

00:22:06.851 --> 00:22:08.280
don't want to have to crash,

00:22:08.281 --> 00:22:09.710
if the master crashes,

00:22:09.711 --> 00:22:10.870
we don't want to replica have to

00:22:10.871 --> 00:22:12.260
take five minutes to
get caught backup

00:22:12.261 --> 00:22:13.610
because now that's downtime

00:22:13.611 --> 00:22:14.990
we have to include in our system.

00:22:14.991 --> 00:22:15.670
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]

00:22:15.671 --> 00:22:17.770
&gt;&gt; Yes. Perfect. So,
there's a bunch of

00:22:17.771 --> 00:22:20.145
other stuff you have to
consider for this like can you,

00:22:20.146 --> 00:22:21.210
if you're in a Cloud environment,

00:22:21.211 --> 00:22:22.300
can you scale out additional

00:22:22.301 --> 00:22:23.810
BMs to collect more
training data.

00:22:23.811 --> 00:22:25.210
How do you generate
the workloads,

00:22:25.211 --> 00:22:27.010
so you're the input sequence

00:22:27.011 --> 00:22:28.830
to the replicas are different?

00:22:28.831 --> 00:22:30.640
Right? So, there's a whole bunch
of assistance questions

00:22:30.641 --> 00:22:32.755
to actually make this work
and I think it's interesting.

00:22:32.756 --> 00:22:35.690
So, to finish up let's
talk about Oracle.

00:22:35.691 --> 00:22:38.260
You might have seen
this announcement last year,

00:22:38.261 --> 00:22:40.000
were Oracle came out and says,

00:22:40.001 --> 00:22:41.590
"We have the world's first

00:22:41.591 --> 00:22:43.380
self-driving Database
Management System".

00:22:43.381 --> 00:22:45.550
Larry Ellison got on
stage and he said,

00:22:45.551 --> 00:22:47.010
"This is the most
important thing that

00:22:47.011 --> 00:22:48.680
the Oracle Corporation has

00:22:48.681 --> 00:22:50.600
worked on in the last 20 years".

00:22:50.601 --> 00:22:53.060
So, the other thing about
this announcement is

00:22:53.061 --> 00:22:56.730
that we had a paper in January,

00:22:56.731 --> 00:22:58.170
a few months before, where we

00:22:58.171 --> 00:23:00.625
talked about our self-driving
database system,

00:23:00.626 --> 00:23:04.180
and at no point did
Larry Ellison mention our work.

00:23:04.181 --> 00:23:06.410
So, I decided to

00:23:06.411 --> 00:23:08.380
shoot him an email and
I'm like "Hey look,

00:23:08.381 --> 00:23:10.080
what's up with that, right?"

00:23:10.081 --> 00:23:12.160
Now, you may be
thinking it's puerile

00:23:12.161 --> 00:23:14.370
for a professor to email
somebody and say "Hey,

00:23:14.371 --> 00:23:15.820
why didn't you cite our work?",

00:23:15.821 --> 00:23:17.630
But Larry Ellison and I

00:23:17.631 --> 00:23:19.450
go pretty far back
and so I thought it

00:23:19.451 --> 00:23:21.190
was okay for me to
email and let them know

00:23:21.191 --> 00:23:23.065
that I was slightly
displeased with this.

00:23:23.066 --> 00:23:26.485
Now, he didn't respond
to this email.

00:23:26.486 --> 00:23:29.395
He never responds to
any of my emails,

00:23:29.396 --> 00:23:31.210
but that's okay we
can still break

00:23:31.211 --> 00:23:32.950
it down and see what
he's actually done.

00:23:32.951 --> 00:23:35.820
So, their self-driving database

00:23:35.821 --> 00:23:36.910
claims that they have for

00:23:36.911 --> 00:23:38.610
key features: They do
automatic indexing,

00:23:38.611 --> 00:23:40.510
recovery, scaling,
and query tuning.

00:23:40.511 --> 00:23:43.080
Now for the first
three items here,

00:23:43.081 --> 00:23:44.910
these are essentially
the same things

00:23:44.911 --> 00:23:46.550
solving the same kind
of problems that

00:23:46.551 --> 00:23:47.960
the self tuning databases we're

00:23:47.961 --> 00:23:50.335
solving in the 2000s, right?

00:23:50.336 --> 00:23:52.860
And so in talking with
the Oracle developers,

00:23:52.861 --> 00:23:54.720
and reading their
marketing material,

00:23:54.721 --> 00:23:57.280
as far as I can tell these
are just the same tools that

00:23:57.281 --> 00:24:00.050
they've been selling before
as DBAs for the DBAs,

00:24:00.051 --> 00:24:02.645
but now just running them
in a managed environment.

00:24:02.646 --> 00:24:05.020
So that means they have
the same limitations or

00:24:05.021 --> 00:24:06.060
problems that I talked about

00:24:06.061 --> 00:24:07.795
before where they
are reactionary,

00:24:07.796 --> 00:24:09.875
and they're not able to
do knowledge transfer.

00:24:09.876 --> 00:24:11.730
So, for this reason,
I don't think this

00:24:11.731 --> 00:24:13.500
is actually really
self-driving databases.

00:24:13.501 --> 00:24:15.050
They're just running your stuff

00:24:15.051 --> 00:24:17.650
automatically and playing tricks,

00:24:17.651 --> 00:24:19.030
the colors to make it look like

00:24:19.031 --> 00:24:21.900
it's fully adaptive
and self-driving.

00:24:21.901 --> 00:24:25.690
So now, for the last one
Automatic Query Tuning,

00:24:25.691 --> 00:24:28.010
ff you're familiar with the
work in academic literature,

00:24:28.011 --> 00:24:30.880
this is usually referred to
as Adaptive Query Processing.

00:24:30.881 --> 00:24:33.430
The idea is if you have
a very expensive Query,

00:24:33.431 --> 00:24:35.310
you run it through the optimizer
when you first start,

00:24:35.311 --> 00:24:36.530
you generate a query plan and

00:24:36.531 --> 00:24:37.770
if you notice over
time as you're running

00:24:37.771 --> 00:24:40.565
it things it's not as good
as you thought it was,

00:24:40.566 --> 00:24:42.830
then you run to
the optimizer again.

00:24:42.831 --> 00:24:45.135
This is not unique to Oracle.

00:24:45.136 --> 00:24:47.090
Microsoft announced last year,

00:24:47.091 --> 00:24:48.490
in Sequel Server 2017,

00:24:48.491 --> 00:24:50.035
that they have the same feature,

00:24:50.036 --> 00:24:52.135
but it's even older
than that, right?

00:24:52.136 --> 00:24:54.320
If you go back into
the early 2000s,

00:24:54.321 --> 00:24:57.450
IBM DB2 had a project called
Leo the Learning Optimizer,

00:24:57.451 --> 00:24:59.405
where they were essentially
doing the same kind of thing,

00:24:59.406 --> 00:25:02.470
but it's even older
than the IBM project.

00:25:02.471 --> 00:25:04.850
It actually goes back to
the 19070s with the first,

00:25:04.851 --> 00:25:07.040
one of the first relational
databases Ingress,

00:25:07.041 --> 00:25:08.420
where they are
essentially, they are

00:25:08.421 --> 00:25:09.805
running the query optimizer,

00:25:09.806 --> 00:25:11.110
over and over again for every

00:25:11.111 --> 00:25:12.415
single Tuple you would examine,

00:25:12.416 --> 00:25:13.650
and they did this not
because they were

00:25:13.651 --> 00:25:15.030
trying to be sophisticated,

00:25:15.031 --> 00:25:16.400
they were doing this because they

00:25:16.401 --> 00:25:18.055
had a really primitive
query optimizer.

00:25:18.056 --> 00:25:19.940
So, again in this case here
there's nothing really

00:25:19.941 --> 00:25:22.355
unique about what Oracle
is claiming they can do,

00:25:22.356 --> 00:25:25.530
and I wouldn't say that this
is self-driving at all.

00:25:25.531 --> 00:25:27.270
What I will say
though, that Oracle is

00:25:27.271 --> 00:25:28.660
in talking with developers,

00:25:28.661 --> 00:25:30.900
they are working on
a newer version of this that

00:25:30.901 --> 00:25:32.105
is certain to incorporate some of

00:25:32.106 --> 00:25:33.905
the things that I'm
talking about here.

00:25:33.906 --> 00:25:36.050
So to finish up, I think that

00:25:36.051 --> 00:25:38.685
autonomous databases are
achievable in the next decade.

00:25:38.686 --> 00:25:40.100
I think it's a lot
of engineering work,

00:25:40.101 --> 00:25:41.350
a lot of systems research,

00:25:41.351 --> 00:25:42.540
and a lot of machine
learning stuff we can

00:25:42.541 --> 00:25:44.835
apply to make this
thing all work.

00:25:44.836 --> 00:25:47.750
I would say for anybody that's
working on a large system,

00:25:47.751 --> 00:25:49.280
they wonder how it would be used,

00:25:49.281 --> 00:25:51.560
whether it's in academia
or in industry.

00:25:51.561 --> 00:25:53.490
Anytime you add a new feature,

00:25:53.491 --> 00:25:55.280
you should not think about how

00:25:55.281 --> 00:25:56.320
that human going to tune this,

00:25:56.321 --> 00:25:57.970
you should really be
thinking about how can

00:25:57.971 --> 00:25:59.640
a machine tune this in
an efficient manner.

00:25:59.641 --> 00:26:01.380
I'm I exposing the right
information and if I have

00:26:01.381 --> 00:26:02.260
the right controls that are

00:26:02.261 --> 00:26:04.020
necessary to make
this thing work.

00:26:04.021 --> 00:26:05.445
So with that I'll finish up

00:26:05.446 --> 00:26:06.950
and I don't think we have
any questions right?

00:26:06.951 --> 00:26:08.275
Because we'll do
a panel afterwards.

00:26:08.276 --> 00:26:10.480
Okay? Thank you.

