00:26:22.151 --> 00:26:23.760
before the memory of the talk

00:26:23.761 --> 00:26:25.890
fades a bit with
the next two talks.

00:26:25.891 --> 00:26:27.310
So if you have any
questions though,

00:26:27.311 --> 00:26:29.390
raise your hand there
are runners who will get

00:26:29.391 --> 00:26:32.770
you a microphone because
these talks are being recorded.

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:38.490
&gt;&gt; Yes. I'll repeat the question
and it makes it easier.

00:26:38.491 --> 00:26:39.775
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:39.776 --> 00:26:42.220
&gt;&gt; Andy, thanks for
the great talk.

00:26:42.221 --> 00:26:44.060
So, you mentioned a bunch of
driving factors like there

00:26:44.061 --> 00:26:46.010
being like available
new hardware and

00:26:46.011 --> 00:26:48.305
available new interesting ways of

00:26:48.306 --> 00:26:51.060
doing more powerful models
such as Steve nuts.

00:26:51.061 --> 00:26:52.800
But from from
my experience one of

00:26:52.801 --> 00:26:54.800
the big differentiators
is also the availability

00:26:54.801 --> 00:26:57.850
of new data or of more data
that allows us to do

00:26:57.851 --> 00:26:59.730
better training and therefore

00:26:59.731 --> 00:27:02.485
build better and much
expressive models.

00:27:02.486 --> 00:27:05.730
So, I'm wondering where do you
get your data from and you

00:27:05.731 --> 00:27:08.730
have any interesting
insights of how

00:27:08.731 --> 00:27:11.580
to do better and more
