better models in this context?

00:27:14.661 --> 00:27:16.565
&gt;&gt; So, his question is that,

00:27:16.566 --> 00:27:18.540
so Yohannes is arguing that

00:27:18.541 --> 00:27:20.980
the having a lot of training data

00:27:20.981 --> 00:27:23.360
is the key thing to
having models that

00:27:23.361 --> 00:27:26.555
actually we can derive use from.

00:27:26.556 --> 00:27:28.895
So, what are some things
we can do to

00:27:28.896 --> 00:27:32.050
improve the collection in
training data? Is that correct?

00:27:32.051 --> 00:27:32.535
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:27:32.536 --> 00:27:35.570
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, the one thing I

00:27:35.571 --> 00:27:39.345
said before is about
this example here,

00:27:39.346 --> 00:27:41.070
if you use your
replicas to try out

00:27:41.071 --> 00:27:42.200
different configurations this is

00:27:42.201 --> 00:27:43.535
collecting more training data.

00:27:43.536 --> 00:27:46.000
But this this is helping you to

00:27:46.001 --> 00:27:47.370
converge to see whether
you're actually getting

00:27:47.371 --> 00:27:49.580
benefit from
the choices you make.

00:27:49.581 --> 00:27:54.400
In terms of the actual
metrics I think that,

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:59.425
the systems are doing
monitoring now,

00:27:59.426 --> 00:28:01.460
we just make sure
that we push them

00:28:01.461 --> 00:28:03.490
out and build our models
based on them.

00:28:03.491 --> 00:28:05.050
So, there's not really
anything in terms

00:28:05.051 --> 00:28:07.630
of what metrics you collect,

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:10.340
not really anything
in there that's

00:28:10.341 --> 00:28:12.165
different than what
existing systems do now.

00:28:12.166 --> 00:28:13.305
In the case of Auto-Tune,

00:28:13.306 --> 00:28:14.330
we don't actually need to look at

00:28:14.331 --> 00:28:16.045
the queries or
the actual data itself,

00:28:16.046 --> 00:28:17.570
we just take the metrics,

00:28:17.571 --> 00:28:19.240
the internal performance
counters that

00:28:19.241 --> 00:28:21.535
the system generates
and spits them out.

00:28:21.536 --> 00:28:24.710
The other thing I'll say
though is a lot of times

00:28:24.711 --> 00:28:26.490
developers just add whenever

00:28:26.491 --> 00:28:28.090
metric they think off, right?

00:28:28.091 --> 00:28:29.320
So, there's a lot of

00:28:29.321 --> 00:28:31.995
data that it's actually
not really useful.

00:28:31.996 --> 00:28:33.700
So, we have
statistical methods to

00:28:33.701 --> 00:28:35.190
sort of prune out the
things that actually don't

00:28:35.191 --> 00:28:36.800
matter and focus on
the main ones and

00:28:36.801 --> 00:28:40.655
they find the signal and
the noise in that way.

