your environment?

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:01.830
So, this is why divided
my two projects in

00:30:01.831 --> 00:30:03.965
taking existing systems versus

00:30:03.966 --> 00:30:05.915
building the system from scratch.

00:30:05.916 --> 00:30:10.740
For the kind of tuning we
want to hand on Auto-Tune,

00:30:10.741 --> 00:30:12.140
you need to have the source code,

00:30:12.141 --> 00:30:13.850
you need complete control
of everything.

00:30:13.851 --> 00:30:17.540
So, I think for you guys it
will be hard to do this,

00:30:17.541 --> 00:30:19.670
plus SQL server is
a huge code base.

00:30:19.671 --> 00:30:22.330
I think that is something
you guys have to do in-house.

00:30:22.331 --> 00:30:25.090
With Auto-Tune, since
we're just looking at,

00:30:25.091 --> 00:30:28.170
treating the database system
as a black box,

00:30:28.171 --> 00:30:32.160
I think interns are probably
the right way to do this.

00:30:32.161 --> 00:30:36.400
I think that we've

00:30:36.401 --> 00:30:37.600
learned enough about SQL server

00:30:37.601 --> 00:30:39.260
now and other systems
that we come to you guys,

00:30:39.261 --> 00:30:40.635
and say, "Hey if you
add these features",

00:30:40.636 --> 00:30:44.440
but the things we're
going to ask you for,

00:30:44.441 --> 00:30:45.370
is going to taking
longer than you

00:30:45.371 --> 00:30:46.580
can do in a summer internship.

00:30:46.581 --> 00:30:49.020
So, I don't know what
the right approach is for that.

00:30:49.021 --> 00:30:51.645
&gt;&gt; So, I would just add
one small thing that,

00:30:51.646 --> 00:30:53.850
it is true that we get
a lot of telemetric data.

00:30:53.851 --> 00:30:54.010
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:30:54.011 --> 00:30:56.155
&gt;&gt; There is no question.
But as you know,

00:30:56.156 --> 00:31:00.160
we are not allowed to look
at the data or the workload.

00:31:00.161 --> 00:31:03.555
So, even though there is a lot of

00:31:03.556 --> 00:31:05.460
data in our Cloud service

00:31:05.461 --> 00:31:08.630
somewhere we're not
entitled to look at it,

00:31:08.631 --> 00:31:11.510
that query string or actual data,

00:31:11.511 --> 00:31:13.830
unless the customer has raised

00:31:13.831 --> 00:31:16.290
a ticket and for
a limited time and audited,

00:31:16.291 --> 00:31:17.915
where somebody looks at it.

00:31:17.916 --> 00:31:19.370
So, it comes with

00:31:19.371 --> 00:31:22.495
the other balancing thing
on our ability to,

00:31:22.496 --> 00:31:25.390
in classical way, look
at all the data like

00:31:25.391 --> 00:31:28.005
Bing or Google can
do, we can't do.

00:31:28.006 --> 00:31:29.510
I think, just wanted to add that.

00:31:29.511 --> 00:31:31.440
&gt;&gt; So, as in aside, I
will say that again,

00:31:31.441 --> 00:31:33.650
I'm not just here
because he's here.

00:31:33.651 --> 00:31:35.505
Just the way that AutoAdmin was

00:31:35.506 --> 00:31:38.370
the leader in this area
in the 2000s,

00:31:38.371 --> 00:31:40.650
in my visits with companies,

00:31:40.651 --> 00:31:42.465
I think Microsoft again,
in the Cloud area,

00:31:42.466 --> 00:31:44.040
is leading this effort.

00:31:44.041 --> 00:31:46.900
I'm actually surprised
at how primitive some of

00:31:46.901 --> 00:31:48.285
the things that are out there

00:31:48.286 --> 00:31:51.540
in major Cloud vendors
in this area.

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:55.465
&gt;&gt; Well, last question.

00:31:55.466 --> 00:31:58.000
Thanks, Andy. Then,
maybe the last question.

00:31:58.001 --> 00:32:01.695
&gt;&gt; I can ask question.
So Andy, you mentioned-

00:32:01.696 --> 00:32:02.960
&gt;&gt; We can't hear you, but I

00:32:02.961 --> 00:32:04.360
think the microphone is not on.

00:32:04.361 --> 00:32:06.635
I think that microphone.

00:32:06.636 --> 00:32:07.805
&gt;&gt; Can you hear me now?

00:32:07.806 --> 00:32:09.140
&gt;&gt; I can hear you,
I'll just repeat it.

00:32:09.141 --> 00:32:11.600
&gt;&gt; Right. So,
my question was that,

00:32:11.601 --> 00:32:14.360
you mentioned that there was
an increase in the number of

00:32:14.361 --> 00:32:17.455
contradition knobs in
these various database engines,

00:32:17.456 --> 00:32:19.875
by what, a factor of
five over the years.

00:32:19.876 --> 00:32:21.920
But it's likely that
most of the features

00:32:21.921 --> 00:32:24.060
don't really affect the
performance as such,

00:32:24.061 --> 00:32:25.730
in the sense that the same

00:32:25.731 --> 00:32:27.110
three or four
important parameters

00:32:27.111 --> 00:32:28.425
that were there ten years back,

00:32:28.426 --> 00:32:30.685
would probably be the same
ones that you have right now.

00:32:30.686 --> 00:32:31.970
So, actually only the DBA

00:32:31.971 --> 00:32:33.300
needs to look at
those parameters,

00:32:33.301 --> 00:32:35.265
and the rest of them are
just as you have mentioned,

00:32:35.266 --> 00:32:36.810
nice amongst the signal.

00:32:36.811 --> 00:32:38.850
If you are using
machine learning techniques,

00:32:38.851 --> 00:32:40.140
it's likely that
you will introduce

00:32:40.141 --> 00:32:42.020
many new features that
are actually not relevant

00:32:42.021 --> 00:32:43.430
towards the final goal

00:32:43.431 --> 00:32:45.940
of optimizing the
system for performance.

00:32:45.941 --> 00:32:47.920
&gt;&gt; So, his question is that in

00:32:47.921 --> 00:32:50.395
this graph here I'm showing
that there's 100 of knobs.

00:32:50.396 --> 00:32:54.560
But in actuality, most of them
are probably not something

00:32:54.561 --> 00:32:56.640
that a DBA is going
to tune actively

00:32:56.641 --> 00:32:59.910
or tune in a daily job.

00:32:59.920 --> 00:33:02.830
Then, you are saying
that if now we

00:33:02.831 --> 00:33:05.860
start interfacing the data system

00:33:05.861 --> 00:33:08.190
with machinery pieces or
machinery components,

00:33:08.191 --> 00:33:09.790
that is going to
introduce more knobs and

00:33:09.791 --> 00:33:12.275
more complexity, and
how do we handle that?

00:33:12.276 --> 00:33:14.170
So, for the first one,

00:33:14.171 --> 00:33:19.100
I would say that what we
do in Auto-Tune is that,

00:33:19.101 --> 00:33:23.330
we basically run a similar
less of aggression and

00:33:23.331 --> 00:33:25.360
figure out here is
the ranking of the knobs that

00:33:25.361 --> 00:33:27.660
actually matter the most
for a variety of workloads.

00:33:27.661 --> 00:33:30.000
You are right, it
is about 25 or so.

00:33:30.001 --> 00:33:31.340
But I would say that it's

00:33:31.341 --> 00:33:32.960
more than just the
number of knobs,

00:33:32.961 --> 00:33:34.740
it is the dependencies
between them,

00:33:34.741 --> 00:33:39.080
and that just makes the
problem much more difficult.

00:33:39.081 --> 00:33:43.390
The second thing you
said was that adding in

00:33:43.391 --> 00:33:44.850
machine learning parts makes

00:33:44.851 --> 00:33:48.320
this thing even harder to tune.

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:52.070
I would say, yes.

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:55.260
My lofty research goals,

00:33:55.261 --> 00:33:56.885
I think we can omit all of us.

00:33:56.886 --> 00:33:59.010
I don't think we actually
need humans to do very,

00:33:59.011 --> 00:34:01.195
very much other than to give
us a credit card number

00:34:01.196 --> 00:34:04.705
and some initial hints
about how to tune things.

00:34:04.706 --> 00:34:06.260
I don't think there is anything

00:34:06.261 --> 00:34:09.775
that we would need a human
to tune these things for.

00:34:09.776 --> 00:34:11.095
So, that's my goal,

00:34:11.096 --> 00:34:14.930
whether we get there in 10 years,

00:34:14.931 --> 00:34:16.915
I like to think we
can, but we'll see.

00:34:16.916 --> 00:34:19.685
&gt;&gt; Well on that high note,
thank you, Andy.

00:34:19.686 --> 00:34:21.030
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:29.245
&gt;&gt; So, our next speaker is
Professor Tim Krasca from MIT.

00:34:29.246 --> 00:34:31.520
Tim has been in

00:34:31.521 --> 00:34:33.675
our field of delivery system
for a long time,

00:34:33.676 --> 00:34:38.085
and his research has focused
in the past on hybrid,

00:34:38.086 --> 00:34:39.700
I guess in the present as well,

00:34:39.701 --> 00:34:43.529
human-machine delivery systems
and big data management.

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:45.540
Most recently though, he has

00:34:45.541 --> 00:34:47.980
been taking a very
bold agenda which

00:34:47.981 --> 00:34:52.050
is to go and look at the
database systems architecture,

00:34:52.051 --> 00:34:55.380
which is not exactly young,

00:34:55.381 --> 00:34:59.410
and say that maybe using data and

00:34:59.411 --> 00:35:02.170
machine learning techniques
we can take a hard look at

00:35:02.171 --> 00:35:05.970
these component and see
how can be architected?

00:35:05.971 --> 00:35:07.200
How can they be even better

00:35:07.201 --> 00:35:09.520
than what it has
been traditionally?

00:35:09.521 --> 00:35:12.375
I think we are going to
hear some of that today.

00:35:12.376 --> 00:35:15.785
I also wanted to say that
he has had many awards,

00:35:15.786 --> 00:35:16.910
I have mentioned some of them,

00:35:16.911 --> 00:35:19.450
he has IEEE Data Engineering
Best Paper Award in

00:35:19.451 --> 00:35:24.005
2013 and the Sloan
Fellowship Award 2017,

00:35:24.006 --> 00:35:25.950
and most recently
he is the winner of

00:35:25.951 --> 00:35:29.975
the 2018 VLDB Early Career
Research Contributions Award.

00:35:29.976 --> 00:35:31.290
With that, Tim take it away.

00:35:31.291 --> 00:35:31.635
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:35:31.636 --> 00:35:32.600
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:35:32.601 --> 00:35:35.245
&gt;&gt; Thanks. Can you all hear me?

00:35:35.246 --> 00:35:36.860
In the back as well?
It's all good?

00:35:36.861 --> 00:35:38.370
Perfect. So, what I am

00:35:38.371 --> 00:35:40.060
going to talk about is
actually a paper we

00:35:40.061 --> 00:35:42.040
published this year in SIGMOD

00:35:42.041 --> 00:35:45.010
called The Case for
Learned Index Structures.

00:35:45.011 --> 00:35:48.810
Half of the talk, if you
have seen it maybe before,

00:35:48.811 --> 00:35:50.200
particularly the
Microsoft people,

00:35:50.201 --> 00:35:51.395
you know it already.

00:35:51.396 --> 00:35:53.530
However, I also added
some new stuff with it on

00:35:53.531 --> 00:35:56.330
multidimensional indexes
for the second half.

00:35:56.331 --> 00:35:59.700
So, you can fall asleep
for the first 10 minutes.

00:35:59.701 --> 00:36:02.620
The work we published in

00:36:02.621 --> 00:36:06.635
SIGMOD mode actually
went viral last year.

00:36:06.636 --> 00:36:08.330
It all started with a tweet

00:36:08.331 --> 00:36:09.830
by Christopher Manning
saying that

00:36:09.831 --> 00:36:13.325
"Machine Learning Just Ate
Algorithms In One Large Bite".

00:36:13.326 --> 00:36:15.985
I just want to give
a disclaimer here up front,

00:36:15.986 --> 00:36:18.380
we by no means believe that

00:36:18.381 --> 00:36:20.790
machine learning has eaten

00:36:20.791 --> 00:36:23.590
the whole field of algorithms
and data structures.

00:36:23.591 --> 00:36:26.670
However, we do believe maybe
it has taken a little bit

00:36:26.671 --> 00:36:28.050
from it and there's a lot

00:36:28.051 --> 00:36:29.800
of more interesting
work to be done.

00:36:29.801 --> 00:36:32.790
So, why are people
so excited about it?

00:36:32.791 --> 00:36:34.750
The fundamental building
blocks essentially,

00:36:34.751 --> 00:36:37.815
of all systems in particular
to data management system,

00:36:37.816 --> 00:36:39.880
are data structure
and algorithms.

00:36:39.881 --> 00:36:41.540
We have a whole range
of really of

00:36:41.541 --> 00:36:42.880
this fundamental data structures

00:36:42.881 --> 00:36:45.620
from different
sorting algorithms,

00:36:45.621 --> 00:36:47.915
HashMaps, different
types of indexes,

00:36:47.916 --> 00:36:50.100
priority queues and so on.

00:36:50.101 --> 00:36:53.150
All these data structures
have in common that they

00:36:53.151 --> 00:36:56.110
essentially make
no assumptions about the data.

00:36:56.111 --> 00:36:59.245
So, now let me give you
just a very simple example.

00:36:59.246 --> 00:37:02.405
Let's assume that in
your database system,

00:37:02.406 --> 00:37:03.990
you want to store and

00:37:03.991 --> 00:37:08.025
carry all integers
from 900-800 million.

00:37:08.026 --> 00:37:10.060
You are interested in
range queries for them,

00:37:10.061 --> 00:37:13.330
you are looking into
scanning for example,

00:37:13.331 --> 00:37:15.150
all the integers from 100 to

00:37:15.151 --> 00:37:17.235
something and
their coding records.

00:37:17.236 --> 00:37:19.925
So, very simple example.

00:37:19.926 --> 00:37:21.895
In this particular case,

00:37:21.896 --> 00:37:24.930
probably nobody here would
actually use a B-tree for it

00:37:24.931 --> 00:37:26.830
because it's you know that

00:37:26.831 --> 00:37:29.225
has all the integers
in that range.

00:37:29.226 --> 00:37:32.600
Instead, what you
could do is from

00:37:32.601 --> 00:37:34.430
an index structure just

00:37:34.431 --> 00:37:36.555
like the lookup key
itself as an offset.

00:37:36.556 --> 00:37:39.160
So what you do is just
lookup into your datastore.

00:37:39.161 --> 00:37:42.200
The Lookup key minus 900,
which gives you the offset,

00:37:42.201 --> 00:37:43.710
and you can then
immediately start

00:37:43.711 --> 00:37:45.500
reading because you know

00:37:45.501 --> 00:37:47.210
something about
the data distribution.

00:37:47.211 --> 00:37:48.990
So, now assume that you store

00:37:48.991 --> 00:37:50.560
all even integer numbers from

00:37:50.561 --> 00:37:55.045
900-800 million and
the same trick still works.

00:37:55.046 --> 00:37:57.580
You take your lookup key
minus 900 divided by two,

00:37:57.581 --> 00:37:58.865
which gives you again the offset

00:37:58.866 --> 00:38:00.540
and you can look
it immediately up.

00:38:00.541 --> 00:38:02.970
So, what we did here
is essentially,

00:38:02.971 --> 00:38:04.400
we had the B-tree before,

00:38:04.401 --> 00:38:06.935
which has a lock and lookup time.

00:38:06.936 --> 00:38:08.860
So, you need to
traverse this and now

00:38:08.861 --> 00:38:11.975
transform it to something
that is essentially over.

00:38:11.976 --> 00:38:14.425
This still holds true for

00:38:14.426 --> 00:38:17.020
other data distributions as long

00:38:17.021 --> 00:38:19.000
as you know the empirical
data distribution and

00:38:19.001 --> 00:38:21.310
can compactly represent it.

00:38:21.311 --> 00:38:24.720
So, the key insight
is that if you take

00:38:24.721 --> 00:38:26.505
this black box of the data

00:38:26.506 --> 00:38:29.050
and now just making
the black box by saying,

00:38:29.051 --> 00:38:30.460
okay, if you know something about

00:38:30.461 --> 00:38:31.905
your empirical data distribution,

00:38:31.906 --> 00:38:33.545
about the data you store,

00:38:33.546 --> 00:38:35.140
you can put much,

00:38:35.141 --> 00:38:36.870
much better data
structures for them.

00:38:36.871 --> 00:38:40.755
In some cases, even transform
the complexity class.

00:38:40.756 --> 00:38:44.050
Now obviously, it's not efficient

00:38:44.051 --> 00:38:45.270
to build one system from

00:38:45.271 --> 00:38:47.480
scratch for every single
used case you have.

00:38:47.481 --> 00:38:49.240
This is not economical,

00:38:49.241 --> 00:38:51.280
but this is exactly where

00:38:51.281 --> 00:38:53.690
machine learning comes in
because it provides us

00:38:53.691 --> 00:38:55.730
the toolbox to learn these types

00:38:55.731 --> 00:38:58.500
of models and then take
afterwards advantage of them.

00:38:58.501 --> 00:39:02.330
So, here I will use
B-trees as a main example,

00:39:02.331 --> 00:39:04.810
and just for
simplicity right now,

00:39:04.811 --> 00:39:09.395
I assume that we have
an in-memory immutable B-tree.

00:39:09.396 --> 00:39:11.750
There's no inserts and no paging.

00:39:11.751 --> 00:39:13.890
Just for simplification, I will

00:39:13.891 --> 00:39:16.815
talk about inserts
and paging later on.

00:39:16.816 --> 00:39:19.660
So, if you think what
a B-tree actually does is,

00:39:19.661 --> 00:39:21.885
given a key, the B-tree

00:39:21.886 --> 00:39:24.660
finds you the position
inside the sorted array.

00:39:24.661 --> 00:39:27.770
Normally, we page
the sorted array,

00:39:27.771 --> 00:39:29.280
meaning that we put it into

00:39:29.281 --> 00:39:31.640
different pages, and
then for example,

00:39:31.641 --> 00:39:34.225
your only index is the first
key inside the page,

00:39:34.226 --> 00:39:36.430
just for efficiency reasons

00:39:36.431 --> 00:39:38.330
that the B-tree doesn't
blow entirely up.

00:39:38.331 --> 00:39:39.910
So, in that sense,

00:39:39.911 --> 00:39:42.085
what a B-tree does given the key,

00:39:42.086 --> 00:39:43.415
it finds me the page.

00:39:43.416 --> 00:39:44.990
Then, I need to search inside

00:39:44.991 --> 00:39:47.010
the page to find the extra key.

00:39:47.011 --> 00:39:49.535
So, it's already
an approximate data structure

00:39:49.536 --> 00:39:51.075
because given a key,

00:39:51.076 --> 00:39:52.560
you get the position inside

00:39:52.561 --> 00:39:54.290
the sorted array
and then you have

00:39:54.291 --> 00:39:57.400
to search inside the page size.

00:39:57.401 --> 00:39:59.865
So, it's a guarantee that
you will find it there.

00:39:59.866 --> 00:40:02.650
In that sense, I can actually

00:40:02.651 --> 00:40:06.080
replace the B-tree with a model,

00:40:06.081 --> 00:40:07.920
as long as I have the right key

00:40:07.921 --> 00:40:09.940
and the model predicts
me the position where

00:40:09.941 --> 00:40:13.925
this key might be inside
the assorted array, I'm good.

00:40:13.926 --> 00:40:15.860
Now, the question is
just like the first,

00:40:15.861 --> 00:40:18.130
how do I still get
the guarantee that I can still

00:40:18.131 --> 00:40:21.030
find my key inside
the sorted array? Where is it?

00:40:21.031 --> 00:40:22.790
Like in the previous case,

00:40:22.791 --> 00:40:24.280
the page defined

00:40:24.281 --> 00:40:26.695
the mini-max error of
where to find the key.

00:40:26.696 --> 00:40:29.445
First of all, it's actually
surprisingly simple.

00:40:29.446 --> 00:40:32.905
So, let's assume I have a
monotonically increasing model,

00:40:32.906 --> 00:40:34.080
what that means is just,

00:40:34.081 --> 00:40:35.750
I can run every key through

00:40:35.751 --> 00:40:38.435
the model and then look
on how far it is off,

00:40:38.436 --> 00:40:40.915
and simply remember the maximum

00:40:40.916 --> 00:40:42.550
over and under prediction,

00:40:42.551 --> 00:40:44.475
which gives me
a strong error bond.

00:40:44.476 --> 00:40:47.265
In the end, it sorts out
but we don't even need that

00:40:47.266 --> 00:40:49.790
because given that the whole
thing is sorted anyway,

00:40:49.791 --> 00:40:51.760
I get a prediction and then
I can do something like

00:40:51.761 --> 00:40:53.200
exponential search
to find actually

00:40:53.201 --> 00:40:54.750
the key right in place,

00:40:54.751 --> 00:40:58.010
wherever it is, without
needing to search everything.

00:40:59.010 --> 00:41:02.710
I can get the bound and
then with the bound,

00:41:02.711 --> 00:41:04.610
I could do binary search,
if I don't have the bound,

00:41:04.611 --> 00:41:06.795
I could do something
like exponential steps.

00:41:06.796 --> 00:41:09.620
So, it turns out what
we're actually doing here,

00:41:09.621 --> 00:41:13.155
is the index, is like
modeling the CDF of the data.

00:41:13.156 --> 00:41:14.865
So, given a key,

00:41:14.866 --> 00:41:17.145
it finds me the position
inside the sorted array,

00:41:17.146 --> 00:41:20.240
which is just nothing else
than trying to estimate

00:41:20.241 --> 00:41:21.640
the probability mass of

00:41:21.641 --> 00:41:23.000
every key which is

00:41:23.001 --> 00:41:25.120
equal or less than the
key I'm looking up,

00:41:25.121 --> 00:41:27.390
times, and the numbers
of keys I have in total,

00:41:27.391 --> 00:41:30.080
which gives me the position
inside the sorted array.

00:41:30.081 --> 00:41:32.115
This insight is
interesting because

00:41:32.116 --> 00:41:33.820
this allows us to
take advantage of

00:41:33.821 --> 00:41:35.540
this whole literature out there

00:41:35.541 --> 00:41:39.025
on how CDFs can be modeled.

00:41:39.026 --> 00:41:42.155
Even more interesting, is that

00:41:42.156 --> 00:41:45.275
a B-tree itself can also be
seen as a model already.

00:41:45.276 --> 00:41:47.340
It's a form of a regression tree,

00:41:47.341 --> 00:41:50.915
and it's just like a special
form of a special structure,

00:41:50.916 --> 00:41:52.300
but it's also a model.

00:41:52.301 --> 00:41:53.460
So, there's nothing that even,

00:41:53.461 --> 00:41:55.580
but we do right now you can
actually see as models,

00:41:55.581 --> 00:41:58.390
I'm just saying you can
use other things as well.

00:41:58.391 --> 00:42:00.110
So, what does it mean,

00:42:00.111 --> 00:42:03.510
and that makes potentially
some people here very happy,

00:42:03.511 --> 00:42:05.490
that is database people
were actually the

00:42:05.491 --> 00:42:07.770
very first to do large-scale
Machine Learning.

00:42:07.771 --> 00:42:11.265
Right? Because we were
the first to do large B-trees,

00:42:11.266 --> 00:42:14.375
which is nothing else
than building a model.

00:42:14.376 --> 00:42:16.875
So, what might be
the potential of using

00:42:16.876 --> 00:42:20.530
other models than the normal
regression trees or B-trees?

00:42:20.531 --> 00:42:22.155
There are a whole bunch of them.

00:42:22.156 --> 00:42:24.500
First of all, they might lead to

00:42:24.501 --> 00:42:27.370
smaller indexes if for example,

00:42:27.371 --> 00:42:29.830
in the case before, if we know
we have all the integers,

00:42:29.831 --> 00:42:32.620
I can build a much more
compact representation of it.

00:42:32.621 --> 00:42:34.495
The only thing I need
to select the offset.

00:42:34.496 --> 00:42:36.880
Maybe an intercept, and
the slope, and I'm done.

00:42:36.881 --> 00:42:39.685
It's much more space efficient.

00:42:39.686 --> 00:42:41.510
I might get faster lookups.

00:42:41.511 --> 00:42:43.224
I might get more parallelism,

00:42:43.225 --> 00:42:45.930
and that is mainly
because for many models,

00:42:45.931 --> 00:42:48.705
I'm transforming the
very heavy if statements

00:42:48.706 --> 00:42:50.299
B-tree needs to traverse

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:53.370
into multiplications
and additions.

00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:56.080
I might be able to

00:42:56.081 --> 00:42:57.905
take advantage of
hardware accelerators,

00:42:57.906 --> 00:42:59.570
and we heard in
the morning already that

00:42:59.571 --> 00:43:01.350
there's a lot of
excitement going on

00:43:01.351 --> 00:43:04.865
in building FPGAs especially
for Machine Learning.

00:43:04.866 --> 00:43:06.805
So, now, by transforming them,

00:43:06.806 --> 00:43:08.825
I might have been advantaged

00:43:08.826 --> 00:43:12.735
to actually leverage them
also for database systems.

00:43:12.736 --> 00:43:16.020
There's also a chance of
having a shipper inserts,

00:43:16.021 --> 00:43:17.520
I will talk about that later.

00:43:17.521 --> 00:43:20.630
So, we tried that out very early

00:43:20.631 --> 00:43:23.600
on like after having
this observation in TensorFlow.

00:43:23.601 --> 00:43:26.240
So, we built a TensorFlow ReLU,

00:43:26.241 --> 00:43:31.025
two layer fully connected,
certitude, ReLU activated.

00:43:31.026 --> 00:43:33.905
We treated over 200 million
web-server log records,

00:43:33.906 --> 00:43:35.650
we wanted to do
index a timestamp,

00:43:35.651 --> 00:43:38.020
and essentially the goal

00:43:38.021 --> 00:43:39.130
of the integrals was
giving me a times,

00:43:39.131 --> 00:43:42.410
and it gave me the position
inside the sorted array.

00:43:42.411 --> 00:43:45.835
So, a cash optimized
B-tree for this task,

00:43:45.836 --> 00:43:48.435
roughly takes 250 nanoseconds.

00:43:48.436 --> 00:43:52.020
Any guesses how long
