00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:58.210
The first attempt 80,000.

00:43:58.211 --> 00:44:00.130
So, this successfully we made

00:44:00.131 --> 00:44:02.070
just almost three
orders of magnitudes,

00:44:02.071 --> 00:44:03.660
a little bit more slower,

00:44:03.661 --> 00:44:05.630
and it's not very successful.

00:44:05.631 --> 00:44:08.080
The reason for them
are very manifold.

00:44:08.081 --> 00:44:09.460
First of all, TensorFlow is

00:44:09.461 --> 00:44:10.870
designed for very large models,

00:44:10.871 --> 00:44:12.540
not for these tiny
things which are

00:44:12.541 --> 00:44:15.400
supposed to run in
the range of nanoseconds.

00:44:15.401 --> 00:44:18.070
The search doesn't take advantage

00:44:18.071 --> 00:44:20.160
that we actually get pretty
close by the prediction,

00:44:20.161 --> 00:44:23.175
but often our minimax error
is very, very large.

00:44:23.176 --> 00:44:25.260
It's like most of the time
we actually close by,

00:44:25.261 --> 00:44:28.100
so if you binary search
this throws you off.

00:44:28.101 --> 00:44:31.165
Then B-trees are very
cache efficient.

00:44:31.166 --> 00:44:32.360
So, if you have a large model,

00:44:32.361 --> 00:44:34.035
you lose that as well, plus

00:44:34.036 --> 00:44:35.750
they are great for overfitting.

00:44:35.751 --> 00:44:37.410
In this case, overfitting,

00:44:37.411 --> 00:44:38.815
as long as you only have

00:44:38.816 --> 00:44:41.370
lookups and all inserts
is actually a good thing,

00:44:41.371 --> 00:44:42.770
because as more you overfit,

00:44:42.771 --> 00:44:44.280
you better look up
your data you have.

00:44:44.281 --> 00:44:46.810
Here, I am only
addressing the last two,

