00:49:47.461 --> 00:49:48.950
We are looking into query

00:49:48.951 --> 00:49:50.880
optimization, what
we can do there.

00:49:50.881 --> 00:49:52.600
There's a whole range
where you can

00:49:52.601 --> 00:49:54.515
apply very similar ideas.

00:49:54.516 --> 00:49:56.140
Most of the time, it's
all about learning

00:49:56.141 --> 00:49:58.820
the CDF of your data and
taking advantage of that.

00:49:58.821 --> 00:50:01.500
So here, I just want to mention

00:50:01.501 --> 00:50:04.440
something else which is
multidimensional indexes.

00:50:04.441 --> 00:50:07.480
So, our big hope when we
started that actually was not

00:50:07.481 --> 00:50:09.450
to get more efficient

00:50:09.451 --> 00:50:11.355
index structures for
a single dimension.

00:50:11.356 --> 00:50:13.060
Our hope was always like, okay,

00:50:13.061 --> 00:50:15.010
the moment we will
go multidimensional,

00:50:15.011 --> 00:50:17.835
we assumed we would see
the bigger benefits there,

00:50:17.836 --> 00:50:19.620
because with machine
learning in general

00:50:19.621 --> 00:50:21.370
or most of the modes are really,

00:50:21.371 --> 00:50:23.280
really good in, it's
just like capturing

00:50:23.281 --> 00:50:26.390
all the complex relationship
data half, right?

00:50:26.391 --> 00:50:29.220
So, machine learning was

00:50:29.221 --> 00:50:31.530
just like predefined to say like,

00:50:31.531 --> 00:50:32.720
"Okay, it should perform very

00:50:32.721 --> 00:50:34.330
well on multidimensional data."

00:50:34.331 --> 00:50:36.840
The biggest problem we faced in

00:50:36.841 --> 00:50:39.570
the beginning is just
that unfortunately,

00:50:39.571 --> 00:50:42.350
even if we learn
all these complex correlations,

00:50:42.351 --> 00:50:44.290
in the end, there
is only one auto

00:50:44.291 --> 00:50:46.645
on disk, which is
one-dimensional.

00:50:46.646 --> 00:50:49.355
Of course, you can see like
this become more complex,

00:50:49.356 --> 00:50:52.530
but in the end, you have
like one order for scanning.

00:50:52.531 --> 00:50:55.510
This is like big
fundamental limitations

00:50:55.511 --> 00:50:57.350
on how you deal with it
because at some point,

00:50:57.351 --> 00:50:58.470
it doesn't matter how you do it,

00:50:58.471 --> 00:50:59.730
you need to transform
what you have in

00:50:59.731 --> 00:51:02.290
the multidimensional space
to one order on disk,

00:51:02.291 --> 00:51:03.680
and this is like in
the end what matters,

00:51:03.681 --> 00:51:07.030
or one order you
have in main memory.

00:51:07.031 --> 00:51:09.220
So how can you do that?

00:51:09.221 --> 00:51:10.230
So, let's assume those are like

00:51:10.231 --> 00:51:11.830
the different data
points you have.

00:51:11.831 --> 00:51:15.815
Let's assume that this is
maybe some order data.

00:51:15.816 --> 00:51:17.600
You have the order amount
and order Zip Code,

00:51:17.601 --> 00:51:20.365
and those are like two-dimensions
you make queries for.

00:51:20.366 --> 00:51:22.780
One approach you could say okay,

00:51:22.781 --> 00:51:26.505
if the the two attributes are
equally important to you,

00:51:26.506 --> 00:51:28.965
you project them just
through the middle,

00:51:28.966 --> 00:51:31.145
you can see is a form of PCA

00:51:31.146 --> 00:51:33.620
because you give each
the equal weight,

00:51:33.621 --> 00:51:34.900
and then you have one projection

00:51:34.901 --> 00:51:36.050
to a one-dimensional space,

00:51:36.051 --> 00:51:38.000
and this is like the sorting
order you use on disk.

00:51:38.001 --> 00:51:40.520
You can immediately
see in some cases,

00:51:40.521 --> 00:51:41.980
this is like fine, and others,

00:51:41.981 --> 00:51:43.490
this will never work very well.

00:51:43.491 --> 00:51:47.300
For example, if you know
that most of the queries you

00:51:47.301 --> 00:51:49.615
do are for the Order Amount

00:51:49.616 --> 00:51:51.900
and not for the Order Zip Code,

00:51:51.901 --> 00:51:53.200
the best thing you
can actually do

00:51:53.201 --> 00:51:55.180
is you order all your data

00:51:55.181 --> 00:51:57.380
regarding the Order Amount

00:51:57.381 --> 00:51:59.935
and disregard the
Zip Code for that.

00:51:59.936 --> 00:52:01.905
If you mostly lookup
for Zip Codes,

00:52:01.906 --> 00:52:03.335
you would do the opposite.

00:52:03.336 --> 00:52:05.030
You order everything for Zip Code

00:52:05.031 --> 00:52:07.180
but you ignore the Order Amount.

00:52:07.181 --> 00:52:09.790
So, there's a trade-off
in how you actually

00:52:09.791 --> 00:52:11.920
serialize the data and
how you lay it out.

00:52:11.921 --> 00:52:13.750
So, the interesting thing is we

00:52:13.751 --> 00:52:15.715
found that you can
actually even mix them.

00:52:15.716 --> 00:52:17.350
For example, you can say okay,

00:52:17.351 --> 00:52:19.100
I project on to Zip Code,

00:52:19.101 --> 00:52:21.800
then I built larger blocks,

00:52:21.801 --> 00:52:23.585
and though within a block,

00:52:23.586 --> 00:52:25.800
maybe I want to use
a different projection,

00:52:25.801 --> 00:52:26.990
in this case Order Amount.

00:52:26.991 --> 00:52:28.410
So, I first split up into

00:52:28.411 --> 00:52:31.280
Zip Codes and then I project
down to Order Amount.

00:52:31.281 --> 00:52:33.590
So, I have like
every block is now ordered

00:52:33.591 --> 00:52:36.215
within regarding to
the Order Amount.

00:52:36.216 --> 00:52:40.230
And again, I can trade them
off on how they depend,

00:52:40.231 --> 00:52:43.705
and what's best highly
depends on your workload.

00:52:43.706 --> 00:52:45.935
If you never touched
the Zip Code,

00:52:45.936 --> 00:52:47.530
you should probably not order for

00:52:47.531 --> 00:52:50.455
them because it doesn't
provide you any benefit.

00:52:50.456 --> 00:52:53.345
This led to our final design.

00:52:53.346 --> 00:52:55.915
Right now, what we have for
multidimensional indexes,

00:52:55.916 --> 00:52:58.320
is we start out with
the data points we have.

00:52:58.321 --> 00:53:00.795
We have the first model which
is called the 'Projector',

00:53:00.796 --> 00:53:02.230
which based on the workload,

00:53:02.231 --> 00:53:05.510
projects it into an order
we have later on on disk.

00:53:05.511 --> 00:53:07.430
What we do there is exactly

00:53:07.431 --> 00:53:08.880
the trick I showed you before.

00:53:08.881 --> 00:53:10.870
We allow columns on how we

00:53:10.871 --> 00:53:12.980
split it down to this
in this like blocks.

00:53:12.981 --> 00:53:16.280
So we start with the root node
which figures out

00:53:16.281 --> 00:53:17.820
the general direction of the data

00:53:17.821 --> 00:53:20.595
depending on what cruise
they are most common,

00:53:20.596 --> 00:53:23.450
then it projects everything
to this dimension.

00:53:23.451 --> 00:53:26.125
Then we repeat
the process and make

00:53:26.126 --> 00:53:28.135
directions projections for

00:53:28.136 --> 00:53:29.945
all the different blocks
separately again.

00:53:29.946 --> 00:53:31.470
Often they're orthogonal to each

00:53:31.471 --> 00:53:33.900
other just because on
how the data's lay out.

00:53:33.901 --> 00:53:36.610
So we get like for example
these projections down

00:53:36.611 --> 00:53:39.490
here that you can see now
like this in every block,

00:53:39.491 --> 00:53:41.330
we have actually
a different order

00:53:41.331 --> 00:53:42.815
of them, and therefore,

00:53:42.816 --> 00:53:45.945
like models of models
again to define or find

00:53:45.946 --> 00:53:50.415
some point in that space
later on on the disk.

00:53:50.416 --> 00:53:52.880
Also interesting, this can be

00:53:52.881 --> 00:53:55.585
recursed so you can make
it as deep as you want.

00:53:55.586 --> 00:53:57.790
And this model itself here is

00:53:57.791 --> 00:53:59.230
the same data structure
that's what I

00:53:59.231 --> 00:54:01.520
showed you before for RMI.

00:54:01.521 --> 00:54:04.590
This is like again,
this expert model on the top

00:54:04.591 --> 00:54:05.700
which picks another expert

00:54:05.701 --> 00:54:07.400
which organizes this differently.

00:54:07.401 --> 00:54:09.970
This, if you have
only linear models,

00:54:09.971 --> 00:54:11.610
can be executed again for

00:54:11.611 --> 00:54:13.425
two-stage models
and two multiplies,

00:54:13.426 --> 00:54:15.290
two additions and
one error lookup.

00:54:15.291 --> 00:54:18.400
So it's extremely fast.

00:54:18.460 --> 00:54:21.335
However, if you
have the Projector,

00:54:21.336 --> 00:54:24.110
it still doesn't give us
now like where to find

00:54:24.111 --> 00:54:26.810
something in a dense array
because still,

00:54:26.811 --> 00:54:28.090
there might be space in between.

00:54:28.091 --> 00:54:29.580
So what we do in order to get

00:54:29.581 --> 00:54:32.020
the dense array is nothing
else than what we had before.

00:54:32.021 --> 00:54:35.975
It's just like build a Locator
again using an RMI model.

00:54:35.976 --> 00:54:38.130
So, this is like essentially,

00:54:38.131 --> 00:54:40.020
we have everything in
one-dimensional space,

00:54:40.021 --> 00:54:41.455
and now, we built like

00:54:41.456 --> 00:54:44.775
the same B-tree model what
we had before all that.

00:54:44.776 --> 00:54:46.925
So, this is pretty new,

00:54:46.926 --> 00:54:50.410
but some very rare initial
performance number

00:54:50.411 --> 00:54:52.425
shows it's pretty promising.

00:54:52.426 --> 00:54:54.880
So, for one million
data points right now,

00:54:54.881 --> 00:54:58.745
you get roughly 200 nanosecond
lookups for point queries.

00:54:58.746 --> 00:55:04.200
This is like a speedup of
two to 10 X over the R-Trees,

00:55:04.201 --> 00:55:06.290
depending on how you
are configure it while

00:55:06.291 --> 00:55:09.430
you have like an order of
magnitude space savings.

00:55:09.530 --> 00:55:11.960
Of course, there's more to come.

00:55:11.961 --> 00:55:13.000
We need more workloads,

00:55:13.001 --> 00:55:14.405
and this is essentially,

00:55:14.406 --> 00:55:16.290
this lookup here is
without considering

00:55:16.291 --> 00:55:18.765
the workload itself yet,

00:55:18.766 --> 00:55:20.340
because we haven't
done that even though,

00:55:20.341 --> 00:55:22.740
it just assumes everything
is uniformly likely,

00:55:22.741 --> 00:55:24.965
which is actually
the worst case scenario for us.

00:55:24.966 --> 00:55:26.760
Also interesting to note is,

00:55:26.761 --> 00:55:28.070
this data structure
in contrast to

00:55:28.071 --> 00:55:29.660
an R-Tree doesn't increase

00:55:29.661 --> 00:55:31.310
with the numbers of
dimensions you have.

00:55:31.311 --> 00:55:34.065
Instead, it should increase
with the rank of the data.

00:55:34.066 --> 00:55:35.930
So, if you have for example,

00:55:35.931 --> 00:55:37.290
at the same attribute again,

00:55:37.291 --> 00:55:38.630
just a full duplicate,

00:55:38.631 --> 00:55:40.490
it wouldn't make any change to

00:55:40.491 --> 00:55:43.270
our layout or the
model complexity.

00:55:43.660 --> 00:55:45.775
So, for future work,

00:55:45.776 --> 00:55:47.625
there's much more to be done,

00:55:47.626 --> 00:55:49.940
we are heavily looking
into sorting and joining.

00:55:49.941 --> 00:55:51.925
So essentially, what we want
to figure out, is just,

00:55:51.926 --> 00:55:54.060
can we not improve
every single component of

00:55:54.061 --> 00:55:56.405
a database system using models,

00:55:56.406 --> 00:55:57.750
and how would that
look like if you

00:55:57.751 --> 00:55:59.605
put in the end
everything together?

00:55:59.606 --> 00:56:01.525
At the core insight is,

00:56:01.526 --> 00:56:03.905
that if you have

00:56:03.906 --> 00:56:07.175
an efficient model about
your data distribution,

00:56:07.176 --> 00:56:08.875
how would you actually build

00:56:08.876 --> 00:56:10.995
your data structure or
algorithms with that?

00:56:10.996 --> 00:56:13.465
You have an Oracle,
which tells you,

00:56:13.466 --> 00:56:16.855
the probability mass given
for a key in your data-set.

00:56:16.856 --> 00:56:18.180
Probably this is Oracle,

00:56:18.181 --> 00:56:20.120
you would design many
of the algorithms and

00:56:20.121 --> 00:56:23.400
data structures you have in
a very, very different way.

00:56:23.820 --> 00:56:27.430
The other thing is, it's all
about continuous functions.

00:56:27.431 --> 00:56:29.170
If you compare that
now, for example,

00:56:29.171 --> 00:56:31.420
to auto preserving
hashing, perfect hashing,

00:56:31.421 --> 00:56:33.300
local sensitive hashing, all the

00:56:33.301 --> 00:56:35.470
different variants
you have, normally,

00:56:35.471 --> 00:56:37.260
they don't take
advantage of the fact

00:56:37.261 --> 00:56:39.200
that you can discontinuous
functions much,

00:56:39.201 --> 00:56:42.225
much better approximate
the rough position,

00:56:42.226 --> 00:56:43.110
so it's like a form of

00:56:43.111 --> 00:56:44.680
interpolation search
but often done with

00:56:44.681 --> 00:56:48.310
regression or more and
more complex shapes.

00:56:48.960 --> 00:56:53.315
If you do that, you have
data structures which learn,

00:56:53.316 --> 00:56:55.270
automatically adapt
to the data you

00:56:55.271 --> 00:56:58.600
have and they're potentially

00:56:58.601 --> 00:57:00.940
run on TPUs or GPUs or FPGAs,

00:57:00.941 --> 00:57:04.090
whatever is your preferred
machine learning platform.

00:57:04.091 --> 00:57:06.690
In some cases, can even lower

00:57:06.691 --> 00:57:10.870
the complexity class for
storage as well as lookup time.

00:57:11.710 --> 00:57:13.880
For example, if you saw that in

00:57:13.881 --> 00:57:15.710
the case of the data area
look up and I

00:57:15.711 --> 00:57:17.150
can also show you
similar things for

00:57:17.151 --> 00:57:19.340
the multidimensional
cases now as well.

00:57:19.341 --> 00:57:21.265
However, just a warning ahead,

00:57:21.266 --> 00:57:23.095
this is not an almighty solution.

00:57:23.096 --> 00:57:24.740
There of course datasets,

00:57:24.741 --> 00:57:26.760
where still a traditional B-Tree
might be better,

00:57:26.761 --> 00:57:28.735
is a lot to be done.

00:57:28.736 --> 00:57:31.845
But it's, I think
a very exciting direction.

00:57:31.846 --> 00:57:35.020
I'm also very happy to
announce that we are starting

00:57:35.021 --> 00:57:38.410
DSAIL for data systems
for AI Lab at MIT,

00:57:38.411 --> 00:57:42.240
and that Microsoft is one of
our major sponsors for it.

00:57:42.241 --> 00:57:44.420
It involves half systems people,

00:57:44.421 --> 00:57:46.660
half ML people from both sides

00:57:46.661 --> 00:57:49.350
and the key questions we
are investigating is,

00:57:49.351 --> 00:57:50.730
how we can use
machine learning for

00:57:50.731 --> 00:57:53.385
systems or build systems
for machine learning?

00:57:53.386 --> 00:57:56.305
With that, I think I'am
more or less in time.

00:57:56.306 --> 00:57:57.630
I'd like to conclude.

00:57:57.631 --> 00:58:00.085
So, what I showed you is
a new approach for indexing.

00:58:00.086 --> 00:58:01.810
I think it's
a framework to rethink

00:58:01.811 --> 00:58:04.930
many existing data structures
and algorithms and there's

00:58:04.931 --> 00:58:07.865
certain conditions that might
change the complexity class

00:58:07.866 --> 00:58:09.320
for a particular instance of

00:58:09.321 --> 00:58:11.335
the data that's always
important to say,

00:58:11.336 --> 00:58:13.710
and the idea might
have implications

00:58:13.711 --> 00:58:17.290
within an outside database
systems. Thank you.

00:58:21.360 --> 00:58:29.500
&gt;&gt; Any questions?
There is a question.

00:58:29.501 --> 00:58:34.220
Peter just, let me
get you a microphone.

00:58:34.740 --> 00:58:38.360
&gt;&gt; I can also repeat
it, it's fine.

00:58:41.430 --> 00:58:47.140
&gt;&gt; Thank you. So, can

00:58:47.141 --> 00:58:48.390
you explain a little bit
about what it

00:58:48.391 --> 00:58:50.615
looks like when you're
actually learning

00:58:50.616 --> 00:58:55.085
these models versus the process
of building a B-tree?

00:58:55.086 --> 00:58:57.520
So, if you're doing

00:58:57.521 --> 00:59:00.550
dynamically maintaining
these things on the fly,

00:59:00.551 --> 00:59:04.505
it's a little unclear from
your talk what it looks like?

00:59:04.506 --> 00:59:07.405
If it gives you a big blob
of data, what do you do?

00:59:07.406 --> 00:59:10.780
&gt;&gt; Right. To create an indexer,

00:59:10.781 --> 00:59:12.130
what I mainly talked to you about

00:59:12.131 --> 00:59:14.090
was only read-only data.

00:59:14.091 --> 00:59:16.190
So, the simplest thing is just,

00:59:16.191 --> 00:59:19.530
you sort your data and then
if you use for example,

00:59:19.531 --> 00:59:21.715
the two-stage linear model,

00:59:21.716 --> 00:59:25.200
the top model you can train
in one pass over the data.

00:59:25.201 --> 00:59:26.680
Actually you don't even
need the full data,

00:59:26.681 --> 00:59:28.170
you can skip pieces of it because

00:59:28.171 --> 00:59:30.375
the sample is often
more than enough.

00:59:30.376 --> 00:59:32.110
So, it's just one pass

00:59:32.111 --> 00:59:33.640
over it because
it's a closed form,

00:59:33.641 --> 00:59:36.060
there's no gradient
descent or anything.

00:59:36.061 --> 00:59:38.570
Then, what you use is,
you use the top model,

00:59:38.571 --> 00:59:40.885
go over all the data
points you have,

00:59:40.886 --> 00:59:43.010
put every data point
into the bucket of

00:59:43.011 --> 00:59:45.360
what expert it belongs
one level down,

00:59:45.361 --> 00:59:47.300
this is our greedy
training approach and

00:59:47.301 --> 00:59:49.890
then for every bucket
you repeat the process.

00:59:49.891 --> 00:59:54.050
So, you go over the data
as many stages you have.

00:59:54.051 --> 00:59:55.580
So, if you have
two stitch models,

00:59:55.581 --> 00:59:56.910
you have two passes
over the data.

00:59:56.911 --> 00:59:58.080
If you have three stitch models,

00:59:58.081 --> 01:00:00.075
three passes over
the data, and so on.

01:00:00.076 --> 01:00:02.520
It goes extremely fast.

01:00:02.521 --> 01:00:06.635
I mean, a B-tree you can build
in one pass over the data,

01:00:06.636 --> 01:00:07.805
if everything is sorted

01:00:07.806 --> 01:00:12.750
but the first one doesn't
work on a sample.

01:00:12.751 --> 01:00:16.750
Inserts, there's another paper
by Casten and some of

01:00:16.751 --> 01:00:18.935
my collaborators
at Brown where we

01:00:18.936 --> 01:00:21.220
look into on how we can do
inserts more efficiently.

01:00:21.221 --> 01:00:22.440
The easiest way what you do is,

01:00:22.441 --> 01:00:24.005
you have a delta index,

01:00:24.006 --> 01:00:26.470
instead of doing the inserts
every single time,

01:00:26.471 --> 01:00:28.560
retraining them all, you put
them in a delta and then

01:00:28.561 --> 01:00:30.810
at some point you figure out
if it's lost by retraining,

01:00:30.811 --> 01:00:32.820
or merging, or whatever.

01:00:37.830 --> 01:00:42.650
&gt;&gt; There's a question
back there at the end,

01:00:45.030 --> 01:00:48.915
and then Jen has a question.

01:00:48.916 --> 01:00:50.920
&gt;&gt; So, Tim you're mentioning

01:00:50.921 --> 01:00:52.660
that you'd like to know the CDF's

01:00:52.661 --> 01:00:56.310
of the single column
distributions in advance.

01:00:56.311 --> 01:00:58.630
Isn't that already given to
you by the histograms that

01:00:58.631 --> 01:00:59.780
most database systems already

01:00:59.781 --> 01:01:00.830
maintained for these columns?

01:01:00.831 --> 01:01:02.400
So, I didn't see
you talking about

01:01:02.401 --> 01:01:03.830
the interaction
between histograms

01:01:03.831 --> 01:01:05.225
and the machine learning process.

01:01:05.226 --> 01:01:07.360
&gt;&gt; Right. So histograms are

01:01:07.361 --> 01:01:09.680
in the end still
very coarse-grained.

01:01:09.681 --> 01:01:12.820
So, the question's, if you
would create a histogram with

01:01:12.821 --> 01:01:16.995
the granularity we need for
actual looking the data up,

01:01:16.996 --> 01:01:19.100
then it's a question, how do you

01:01:19.101 --> 01:01:20.960
browse the histogram itself?

01:01:20.961 --> 01:01:22.670
And if you solve that problem,

01:01:22.671 --> 01:01:24.670
actually what you
get is a B-tree.

01:01:24.671 --> 01:01:27.480
So, I can show that you
can transform one into the

01:01:27.481 --> 01:01:30.480
other because you need
the lookup structures over it.

01:01:30.481 --> 01:01:33.415
So, histograms themselves
don't help you that much.

01:01:33.416 --> 01:01:34.970
There's something
like look-up tables

01:01:34.971 --> 01:01:36.380
which was like
the thing in between,

01:01:36.381 --> 01:01:38.000
but I had numbers for them,

01:01:38.001 --> 01:01:39.775
they also don't
help you that much.

01:01:39.776 --> 01:01:41.445
The interesting thing is now,

01:01:41.446 --> 01:01:43.630
let's assume I built
my better model for it,

01:01:43.631 --> 01:01:47.215
let's say that I model
two stage for the index,

01:01:47.216 --> 01:01:51.025
I can use the same
model as my histogram.

01:01:51.026 --> 01:01:54.455
So, instead of having histogram,

01:01:54.456 --> 01:01:55.780
which I cannot use as an index,

01:01:55.781 --> 01:01:57.520
I can use my indexes
as a histogram

01:01:57.521 --> 01:02:00.095
and the performance penalty
is almost not existing.

01:02:00.096 --> 01:02:02.020
Even better, I can use

01:02:02.021 --> 01:02:03.440
the same histograms also to

01:02:03.441 --> 01:02:04.995
plug them into
my query optimizer,

01:02:04.996 --> 01:02:06.200
I can desalting with them.

01:02:06.201 --> 01:02:08.330
Essentially, if I
have one CDF model,

01:02:08.331 --> 01:02:10.130
I can use the same model in many,

01:02:10.131 --> 01:02:12.730
many different places of
my database system from joining,

01:02:12.731 --> 01:02:16.860
sorting, indexing, query
optimization and other things.

01:02:16.861 --> 01:02:19.705
The key challenge
though there is,

01:02:19.706 --> 01:02:22.230
it's very simple
for one dimension,

01:02:22.231 --> 01:02:25.155
it's a little bit harder
for multiple dimensions.

01:02:25.156 --> 01:02:28.280
So then, there's
just conditional probabilities

01:02:28.281 --> 01:02:30.300
and so on what you do there.

01:02:33.520 --> 01:02:37.490
&gt;&gt; Does it work? Back
to Peter's question,

01:02:37.491 --> 01:02:40.505
how about other issues
such as locking,

01:02:40.506 --> 01:02:45.220
logging, compression
and serialization.

01:02:45.900 --> 01:02:48.490
&gt;&gt; Logging, locking,
compression and

01:02:48.491 --> 01:02:50.340
serialization.s Let me start

01:02:50.341 --> 01:02:53.105
with compression because
it's the best story.

01:02:53.106 --> 01:02:56.370
So, let's assume that your index,

01:02:56.371 --> 01:02:58.190
given the key, is gives you

01:02:58.191 --> 01:03:00.640
the position where
to find that key.

01:03:00.641 --> 01:03:02.960
Let's assume my model is

01:03:02.961 --> 01:03:05.745
in a way built that I can
ask the reverse question.

01:03:05.746 --> 01:03:09.725
Given a position, tell me what
key is most likely there.

01:03:09.726 --> 01:03:12.300
If my model would be perfect,

01:03:12.301 --> 01:03:14.045
I don't need my keys anymore.

01:03:14.046 --> 01:03:15.560
It compresses my data,

01:03:15.561 --> 01:03:18.165
essentially, because
given a position,

01:03:18.166 --> 01:03:19.795
I know which key I expect there.

01:03:19.796 --> 01:03:21.590
Why should I store
the keys anymore?

01:03:21.591 --> 01:03:23.715
If the model is not
perfect, what I can do is,

01:03:23.716 --> 01:03:25.070
a delta compress against

01:03:25.071 --> 01:03:26.795
the expected key
at that position.

01:03:26.796 --> 01:03:31.095
So again, the model becomes
my compression technique.

01:03:31.096 --> 01:03:32.925
So, that's compression.

01:03:32.926 --> 01:03:37.370
Locking, there's a big question
about on how you actually

01:03:37.371 --> 01:03:40.710
make B-trees just like

01:03:40.711 --> 01:03:42.920
serializable and everything
as everything else.

01:03:42.921 --> 01:03:44.820
Arios is just the paper everybody

01:03:44.821 --> 01:03:47.750
hates and still everybody uses.

01:03:48.360 --> 01:03:52.565
But, in this way because

01:03:52.566 --> 01:03:54.010
you deal with the inserts in

01:03:54.011 --> 01:03:56.165
a different vein or
approximate fashion anyway,

01:03:56.166 --> 01:03:58.550
my assumption is that many of

01:03:58.551 --> 01:04:02.390
this complexity of locking
is not actually needed.

01:04:02.391 --> 01:04:06.250
I think Microsoft is heavily
looking into that, I heard.

01:04:06.251 --> 01:04:09.315
So, they are very, very
excited about that.

01:04:09.316 --> 01:04:12.990
Logging is like
transaction processing

01:04:12.991 --> 01:04:14.805
and logging is still
something you will need,

01:04:14.806 --> 01:04:16.120
because you want still

01:04:16.121 --> 01:04:18.580
serializability guarantees
and be everything consistent,

01:04:18.581 --> 01:04:20.530
so I doubt that Machine Learning,

01:04:20.531 --> 01:04:22.080
at least I cannot
think of an easy

01:04:22.081 --> 01:04:24.150
way right now to improve that.

01:04:24.151 --> 01:04:26.970
&gt;&gt; Well, with that,

01:04:26.971 --> 01:04:29.245
Tim thanks very much
for an exciting talk.

01:04:29.246 --> 01:04:30.820
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

01:04:34.030 --> 01:04:38.475
Our last speaker for
this session is Matei Zaharia,

01:04:38.476 --> 01:04:42.380
and he's a faculty member
at Stanford University,

01:04:42.381 --> 01:04:45.870
and previously, he
was at MIT faculty.

01:04:45.871 --> 01:04:48.175
During his PhD, as you know,

01:04:48.176 --> 01:04:52.180
he started the Apache Spark
computing engine as well as

01:04:52.181 --> 01:04:53.725
worked on a number of

01:04:53.726 --> 01:04:57.085
open source projects,
such as Mesos.

01:04:57.086 --> 01:04:59.980
Apache Spark turned
into a company

01:04:59.981 --> 01:05:02.220
that landed a founding
called Databricks,

01:05:02.221 --> 01:05:04.850
for which he is the Chief
Technologist as well.

01:05:04.851 --> 01:05:06.260
He has had a number of

01:05:06.261 --> 01:05:09.895
awards: ACM Doctoral
Dissertation Award,

01:05:09.896 --> 01:05:12.400
the VMware Systems
Research Award and

01:05:12.401 --> 01:05:15.625
the Daytona Graysort
world record.

01:05:15.626 --> 01:05:17.810
More recently, he
has been working

01:05:17.811 --> 01:05:19.510
on asking the question,

01:05:19.511 --> 01:05:22.520
to have machine learning
and the data scientists be

01:05:22.521 --> 01:05:24.280
well-supported on

01:05:24.281 --> 01:05:26.870
the data analytic platform,
what needs to happen?

01:05:26.871 --> 01:05:28.650
What are the gaps? And he's

01:05:28.651 --> 01:05:29.720
going to talk about that today.

01:05:29.721 --> 01:05:31.975
So, after having
finished the two talks,

01:05:31.976 --> 01:05:33.880
looking at how ML or

01:05:33.881 --> 01:05:35.030
the analytic techniques could

01:05:35.031 --> 01:05:36.595
help the database internals,

01:05:36.596 --> 01:05:38.420
we're going to shift gear and

01:05:38.421 --> 01:05:40.530
listen to him to see
what we need to do

01:05:40.531 --> 01:05:42.365
to make sure in relation
to the querying

01:05:42.366 --> 01:05:44.605
and other things that
analytic systems give,

01:05:44.606 --> 01:05:46.320
what you have to do
special to make it

01:05:46.321 --> 01:05:48.380
easy for the data
scientist. Matei.

01:05:48.381 --> 01:05:48.870
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:05:48.871 --> 01:05:49.570
&gt;&gt; Take it away.

01:05:49.571 --> 01:05:51.525
&gt;&gt; All right. Thanks, Surajit.

01:05:51.526 --> 01:05:53.100
Let me know if you can't hear me.

01:05:53.101 --> 01:05:55.080
Okay. So, yeah, as Surajit said,

01:05:55.081 --> 01:05:56.870
I'm going to talk
about infrastructure

01:05:56.871 --> 01:05:58.505
for usable machine learning,

01:05:58.506 --> 01:05:59.830
and I'll actually start by

01:05:59.831 --> 01:06:02.140
talking about a bunch of research

01:06:02.141 --> 01:06:03.580
that we're doing in

01:06:03.581 --> 01:06:05.840
a lab on this at
Stanford called DAWN.

01:06:05.841 --> 01:06:07.220
Microsoft is one of

01:06:07.221 --> 01:06:10.315
the generous sponsors of
DAWN, so we appreciate that.

01:06:10.316 --> 01:06:12.680
And then, I'll also
briefly put on

01:06:12.681 --> 01:06:15.800
my industry hat and
talk about a type of

01:06:15.801 --> 01:06:18.400
system that basically
hundreds of organizations are

01:06:18.401 --> 01:06:20.200
building in industry and

01:06:20.201 --> 01:06:21.920
that there isn't a lot
of research about.

01:06:21.921 --> 01:06:23.500
So, I think it's something
that we should look

01:06:23.501 --> 01:06:25.680
at as researchers as well,

01:06:25.681 --> 01:06:28.085
although I am looking
at it in industry.

01:06:28.086 --> 01:06:31.230
So, it's really the golden age
of machine learning.

01:06:31.231 --> 01:06:34.120
There are incredible advances
in image recognition,

01:06:34.121 --> 01:06:37.005
natural language,
planning, yada yada yada.

01:06:37.006 --> 01:06:39.180
It's a great story and
they're starting to

01:06:39.181 --> 01:06:41.755
have societies scale impact
every answered that.

01:06:41.756 --> 01:06:44.440
But if you look at it
a little bit more carefully,

01:06:44.441 --> 01:06:46.900
this statement comes
with a caveat.

01:06:46.901 --> 01:06:48.400
So, it's the golden age of

01:06:48.401 --> 01:06:50.680
machine learning for
the best funded,

01:06:50.681 --> 01:06:52.470
best trained engineering teams.

01:06:52.471 --> 01:06:55.690
If you are a company with
tens of thousands of engineers,

01:06:55.691 --> 01:06:58.210
it's awesome, you're
happy, you can do it;

01:06:58.211 --> 01:07:01.960
but it's still very
difficult for anyone else to

01:07:01.961 --> 01:07:03.720
actually use machine
learning and have

01:07:03.721 --> 01:07:06.445
any kind of real-world impact.

01:07:06.446 --> 01:07:09.440
So, building machine
learning products

01:07:09.441 --> 01:07:11.730
is still very difficult
and very expensive.

01:07:11.731 --> 01:07:13.190
All the major successes,

01:07:13.191 --> 01:07:14.790
the things that actually make it

01:07:14.791 --> 01:07:16.985
into products that
make a difference,

01:07:16.986 --> 01:07:19.460
things like Siri, or Alexa,

01:07:19.461 --> 01:07:23.450
or Autopilot require hundreds
to thousands of engineers

01:07:23.451 --> 01:07:24.480
just working on them

01:07:24.481 --> 01:07:27.800
continuously to build and
maintain those systems.

01:07:27.801 --> 01:07:29.270
And the interesting thing,

01:07:29.271 --> 01:07:30.300
if you look at it is,

01:07:30.301 --> 01:07:31.670
what are those engineers doing?

01:07:31.671 --> 01:07:34.090
Are they all sitting
around a whiteboard doing

01:07:34.091 --> 01:07:36.960
integrals and drawing
neural networks and stuff?

01:07:36.961 --> 01:07:38.690
They're actually not doing that.

01:07:38.691 --> 01:07:40.840
They're not really doing

01:07:40.841 --> 01:07:43.210
the stuff you learn in
a machine learning class.

01:07:43.211 --> 01:07:45.370
Most of the stuff they're
doing that's really

01:07:45.371 --> 01:07:47.410
expensive is data preparation,

01:07:47.411 --> 01:07:50.485
quality assurance, debugging
and productionisation.

01:07:50.486 --> 01:07:54.375
So, that's what is really
needed to feed these systems,

01:07:54.376 --> 01:07:56.045
and it's not modeling.

01:07:56.046 --> 01:07:58.425
And so, just a domain expert in

01:07:58.426 --> 01:07:59.940
a specific area can't

01:07:59.941 --> 01:08:01.955
easily build machine
learning products.

01:08:01.956 --> 01:08:04.500
So, if you look at this from
a research perspective,

01:08:04.501 --> 01:08:05.850
it's actually
a really interesting

01:08:05.851 --> 01:08:06.990
thing to look at because it

01:08:06.991 --> 01:08:10.245
means that the problems that
people have in production,

01:08:10.246 --> 01:08:12.670
any, say, enterprise user
you talk with you ask,

01:08:12.671 --> 01:08:14.120
"Hey, are you using
machine learning?

01:08:14.121 --> 01:08:17.085
What's hard?" The problems
that they face are not

01:08:17.086 --> 01:08:19.645
the problems that machine
learning researchers

01:08:19.646 --> 01:08:20.920
primarily work on.

01:08:20.921 --> 01:08:23.040
Those researchers
work on settings

01:08:23.041 --> 01:08:25.120
where you already have a dataset,

01:08:25.121 --> 01:08:27.110
you already have
a target metric that

01:08:27.111 --> 01:08:29.785
everyone agrees is the
right one to optimize,

01:08:29.786 --> 01:08:32.600
and you don't even care about
productionizing the model,

01:08:32.601 --> 01:08:35.140
you just care about coming
up with a training method;

01:08:35.141 --> 01:08:37.425
and that is not what
anyone actually

01:08:37.426 --> 01:08:39.835
using this stuff
for real has to do.

01:08:39.836 --> 01:08:41.775
So, is this really
a big mismatch where,

01:08:41.776 --> 01:08:44.320
as a systems or
data management researcher,

01:08:44.321 --> 01:08:46.830
you can go in and have an impact.

01:08:46.831 --> 01:08:49.170
We're not the only
ones to say this.

01:08:49.171 --> 01:08:52.190
Another example of a paper

01:08:52.191 --> 01:08:54.580
that says this is actually
this one from Google,

01:08:54.581 --> 01:08:56.420
where they talk about what's

01:08:56.421 --> 01:08:58.540
involved in building these
systems and they say,

01:08:58.541 --> 01:08:59.730
"Only a fraction of

01:08:59.731 --> 01:09:02.140
the real-world ML systems
are the ML code."

01:09:02.141 --> 01:09:03.490
All this other stuff around

01:09:03.491 --> 01:09:05.590
that is what takes a lot of time,

01:09:05.591 --> 01:09:08.515
is expensive, and is hard to do.

01:09:08.516 --> 01:09:11.250
So, in the Stanford DAWN Project,

01:09:11.251 --> 01:09:14.850
it's a project with
three other PIs: Peter Bailis,

01:09:14.851 --> 01:09:17.345
Chris Ré, Kunle and myself.

01:09:17.346 --> 01:09:19.420
We're looking at
specifically this problem:

01:09:19.421 --> 01:09:21.920
all this stuff needed
around the machine learning

01:09:21.921 --> 01:09:24.200
algorithms to build
production applications

01:09:24.201 --> 01:09:26.830
to enable domain experts
to build them.

01:09:26.831 --> 01:09:28.635
And we want to make
it easy to build them

01:09:28.636 --> 01:09:30.440
without the PhD in
machine learning,

01:09:30.441 --> 01:09:33.870
but also importantly without
being an expert in systems,

01:09:33.871 --> 01:09:35.880
and data management and
hardware to get all

01:09:35.881 --> 01:09:38.675
that other infrastructure
around it to work.

01:09:38.676 --> 01:09:41.150
So, in this talk, I'm
going to talk about

01:09:41.151 --> 01:09:43.100
a few pieces of what
we're building in DAWN,

01:09:43.101 --> 01:09:44.380
just to give you examples.

01:09:44.381 --> 01:09:46.690
I won't have time to go
into a lot of detail,

01:09:46.691 --> 01:09:48.400
but just what are these problems

01:09:48.401 --> 01:09:51.175
outside of traditional
machine learning.

01:09:51.176 --> 01:09:52.870
And then, I'll also
talk, as I said,

01:09:52.871 --> 01:09:55.785
about some stuff happening
in industry in this space.

01:09:55.786 --> 01:09:57.945
So, first thing, I'll
talk about, so DAWN,

01:09:57.946 --> 01:10:00.690
we were building the
software Stack that

01:10:00.691 --> 01:10:02.520
spans all these phases

01:10:02.521 --> 01:10:04.725
from data acquisition
to production.

01:10:04.726 --> 01:10:06.970
And the first thing I'll talk
about to give you a sense

01:10:06.971 --> 01:10:10.270
of what these problems
can look like,

01:10:10.271 --> 01:10:13.050
it's actually one of Chris
Ré's projects called Snorkel,

01:10:13.051 --> 01:10:15.630
which is at the data
acquisition phase.

01:10:15.631 --> 01:10:19.360
And this is about acquiring
or using training data.

01:10:19.361 --> 01:10:20.930
So, training data is

01:10:20.931 --> 01:10:23.185
obviously the key to
machine learning,

01:10:23.186 --> 01:10:24.920
and the places where
it's been most

01:10:24.921 --> 01:10:26.600
successful are the places where

01:10:26.601 --> 01:10:28.470
it's very easy and cheap

01:10:28.471 --> 01:10:30.495
to obtain high volume
training data.

01:10:30.496 --> 01:10:34.235
So, image search for images
that occur on the Internet,

01:10:34.236 --> 01:10:36.505
not for things, like
medical images.

01:10:36.506 --> 01:10:38.970
So much data, you can look at it,

01:10:38.971 --> 01:10:40.480
you can build really good models.

01:10:40.481 --> 01:10:42.570
Speech: people are
talking all the time.

01:10:42.571 --> 01:10:44.705
I'm talking right now,
you can record me,

01:10:44.706 --> 01:10:46.080
you could probably build

01:10:46.081 --> 01:10:47.640
a model of what I'm going to say.

01:10:47.641 --> 01:10:49.160
Games: you just play

01:10:49.161 --> 01:10:51.480
some some video games
against yourself,

01:10:51.481 --> 01:10:53.295
that's good, you'll
get lots of data.

01:10:53.296 --> 01:10:54.670
On the other hand, a lot of

01:10:54.671 --> 01:10:57.270
the business applications
that actually matter,

01:10:57.271 --> 01:10:59.540
ones were getting labeled

01:10:59.541 --> 01:11:01.690
data especially, is
quite expensive.

01:11:01.691 --> 01:11:04.035
So, medical images,
you can't just

01:11:04.036 --> 01:11:05.680
google them and see lots of

01:11:05.681 --> 01:11:07.850
x-rays and start
learning from those,

01:11:07.851 --> 01:11:09.590
because there might
not even be that

01:11:09.591 --> 01:11:12.455
many patients that have
a certain disease.

01:11:12.456 --> 01:11:17.230
Document understanding,
you need, say a lawyer,

01:11:17.231 --> 01:11:19.440
to sit down and read
a piece of text and

01:11:19.441 --> 01:11:21.790
tell you what the law
would interpret it like,

01:11:21.791 --> 01:11:23.230
and that person is going to

01:11:23.231 --> 01:11:25.280
cost you thousands
of dollars per hour.

01:11:25.281 --> 01:11:27.520
You can't just get
people to click on

01:11:27.521 --> 01:11:30.065
things for one cent
an hour and so on.

01:11:30.066 --> 01:11:32.660
So, the question in the
Snorkel project is,

01:11:32.661 --> 01:11:34.100
how can we leverage data that's

01:11:34.101 --> 01:11:37.025
expensive for humans
to label at scale?

01:11:37.026 --> 01:11:39.970
And this labeling can
easily be 90 percent of

01:11:39.971 --> 01:11:41.525
the cost in many business

01:11:41.526 --> 01:11:43.385
applications of machine learning.

01:11:43.386 --> 01:11:45.955
So, Chris' project, Snorkel

01:11:45.956 --> 01:11:49.130
basically, looks at
a different interface for

01:11:49.131 --> 01:11:52.395
labeling which is called
Labeling functions.

01:11:52.396 --> 01:11:55.510
So, instead of asking humans
to just give you Labels,

01:11:55.511 --> 01:11:58.530
look at all these examples
and give me a zero or one

01:11:58.531 --> 01:12:02.295
for each one based on
your expertise as a human.

01:12:02.296 --> 01:12:04.340
Despite the Snorkel asked them

01:12:04.341 --> 01:12:06.350
to provide Labeling functions,

01:12:06.351 --> 01:12:08.180
which are short programs

01:12:08.181 --> 01:12:09.730
from a computer
science standpoint

01:12:09.731 --> 01:12:12.270
that can give you

01:12:12.271 --> 01:12:15.045
a guess at a Label but they
may not always be accurate.

01:12:15.046 --> 01:12:16.190
So, for example,

01:12:16.191 --> 01:12:18.720
one thing you could do is
you could sit down with

01:12:18.721 --> 01:12:21.390
say medical doctors and show them

01:12:21.391 --> 01:12:22.770
some notes about patients

01:12:22.771 --> 01:12:24.950
and instead of
asking for each one,

01:12:24.951 --> 01:12:26.745
is this disease or not?

01:12:26.746 --> 01:12:28.130
You can ask them,

01:12:28.131 --> 01:12:29.710
hey what do you look for it that

01:12:29.711 --> 01:12:31.940
lets you think that
this is heart disease?

01:12:31.941 --> 01:12:33.320
Then you can turn that into

01:12:33.321 --> 01:12:34.730
a little Python function like

01:12:34.731 --> 01:12:37.105
searching for X or
something like that.

01:12:37.106 --> 01:12:39.700
Then Snorkel is a training system

01:12:39.701 --> 01:12:41.180
that can take a bunch of

01:12:41.181 --> 01:12:43.645
these functions and
it simultaneously

01:12:43.646 --> 01:12:46.550
learns when each one
is right and wrong,

01:12:46.551 --> 01:12:49.710
how noisy it is and the target
model for the data.

01:12:49.711 --> 01:12:50.790
So, it can incorporate

01:12:50.791 --> 01:12:52.740
this uncertainty from
these functions.

01:12:52.741 --> 01:12:55.140
But the benefit of
this interfaces

01:12:55.141 --> 01:12:56.640
and just by sitting down

01:12:56.641 --> 01:12:58.430
and coding a bunch
of these functions

01:12:58.431 --> 01:13:00.805
after you interview
a domain expert,

01:13:00.806 --> 01:13:03.390
you can then apply
them to millions of

01:13:03.391 --> 01:13:05.745
unlabeled examples and documents.

01:13:05.746 --> 01:13:07.860
So, for example,
Chris's group sat down

01:13:07.861 --> 01:13:10.710
with with researchers in
the medical school at

01:13:10.711 --> 01:13:12.550
Stanford who are
doing these projects

01:13:12.551 --> 01:13:15.049
to automatically
understand document

01:13:15.050 --> 01:13:17.210
or case notes or things
like that and in

01:13:17.211 --> 01:13:19.705
just four hours of
writing stuff with them,

01:13:19.706 --> 01:13:22.080
they would match
basically months or

01:13:22.081 --> 01:13:24.450
years of hand labeling
these poor grad students

01:13:24.451 --> 01:13:26.340
in these fields like part of

01:13:26.341 --> 01:13:27.680
their PhD and sitting down

01:13:27.681 --> 01:13:29.370
for two years to label documents.

01:13:29.371 --> 01:13:33.070
To build an automatic system
for understanding them.

01:13:33.071 --> 01:13:36.460
So, that's kind of the impact
obviously saves a lot of

01:13:36.461 --> 01:13:39.720
time and money and across
a variety of datasets,

01:13:39.721 --> 01:13:42.910
Snorkel can match
basically systems

01:13:42.911 --> 01:13:45.580
that have been trained
on labeled data just

01:13:45.581 --> 01:13:48.190
by using a bunch of these
functions and millions of

01:13:48.191 --> 01:13:51.695
examples that were not labeled
by humans at all before.

01:13:51.696 --> 01:13:54.780
So, this is
a very brief overview.

01:13:54.781 --> 01:13:57.220
Chris actually has
a whole research program around

01:13:57.221 --> 01:13:59.020
this called Data
programming which

01:13:59.021 --> 01:14:01.315
is about automatically prepaying,

01:14:01.316 --> 01:14:03.850
training data as a kind of

01:14:03.851 --> 01:14:07.610
a first-class concept and
it's also all open source.

01:14:07.611 --> 01:14:08.820
So, you can find out about it

01:14:08.821 --> 01:14:11.130
online but this is an example of

01:14:11.131 --> 01:14:12.595
a type of problem that

01:14:12.596 --> 01:14:15.375
real users of machine
learning will face.

01:14:15.376 --> 01:14:18.665
As another example
of a point problem,

01:14:18.666 --> 01:14:21.120
I'll talk about production

01:14:21.121 --> 01:14:23.750
and in particular
serving and I'll talk

01:14:23.751 --> 01:14:25.710
about this project
NoScope which is

01:14:25.711 --> 01:14:28.905
a joint project between
myself and Peter Bailis.

01:14:28.906 --> 01:14:33.380
So, machine-learning models
are very accurate.

01:14:33.381 --> 01:14:38.880
Especially the new deep
learning one in many cases,

01:14:38.881 --> 01:14:41.120
the larger the models
are the more accurate

01:14:41.121 --> 01:14:43.440
they are and so people
want to deploy them.

01:14:43.441 --> 01:14:46.555
But so many times
the actual inference parses

01:14:46.556 --> 01:14:48.120
a is the bottleneck
and it's going to

01:14:48.121 --> 01:14:50.075
be the most expensive part.

01:14:50.076 --> 01:14:52.340
This is why you see
projects like Brainwave

01:14:52.341 --> 01:14:54.895
and so on that try to
accelerate that influence.

01:14:54.896 --> 01:14:57.130
So, in this project
we looked at using

01:14:57.131 --> 01:15:00.130
CNNs to do queries and video,

01:15:00.131 --> 01:15:02.010
something a lot of
people want to do.

01:15:02.011 --> 01:15:03.850
CNNs are really good at

01:15:03.851 --> 01:15:07.010
recognizing and labeling
objects now but the problem

01:15:07.011 --> 01:15:12.230
is these really good CNNs
they're also expensive.

01:15:12.231 --> 01:15:15.755
So, they can process
one video stream in real time

01:15:15.756 --> 01:15:19.735
on a large server class GPU.

01:15:19.736 --> 01:15:21.565
That's what's
considered real time

01:15:21.566 --> 01:15:24.175
inference in the computer
vision community

01:15:24.176 --> 01:15:26.540
and of course if you
have many say millions

01:15:26.541 --> 01:15:29.090
of hours of video are
lots of video streams,

01:15:29.091 --> 01:15:33.200
you don't want to stand
up a huge GPU cluster to

01:15:33.201 --> 01:15:35.400
monitor and even for
a modest-sized building

01:15:35.401 --> 01:15:37.805
this would cost
millions of dollars.

01:15:37.806 --> 01:15:40.450
So, in this project, NoScope
will look at how can you

01:15:40.451 --> 01:15:43.390
get orders of magnitude faster,

01:15:43.391 --> 01:15:46.960
video equates with
a minimal loss in accuracy given

01:15:46.961 --> 01:15:49.340
these black box CNN models

01:15:49.341 --> 01:15:51.850
that you know are really
accurate about expensive.

01:15:51.851 --> 01:15:54.270
So, we use a bunch of
techniques to do this,

01:15:54.271 --> 01:15:58.030
but one of the key techniques
is model specialization.

01:15:58.031 --> 01:16:00.170
So, basically, we're given

01:16:00.171 --> 01:16:02.830
this model that's good
at recognizing things.

01:16:02.831 --> 01:16:05.220
You don't really
necessarily need to know

01:16:05.221 --> 01:16:08.090
what it does internally and
we're also given a query.

01:16:08.091 --> 01:16:09.510
For example, I just want to

01:16:09.511 --> 01:16:11.425
count cars that are driving by

01:16:11.426 --> 01:16:13.480
my building and we're also

01:16:13.481 --> 01:16:15.740
given a bunch of data
to run this query on,

01:16:15.741 --> 01:16:18.360
which as you know
this specific video frames

01:16:18.361 --> 01:16:20.180
on the camera
outside my building.

01:16:20.181 --> 01:16:21.400
So, what we do with this,

01:16:21.401 --> 01:16:22.810
is we use this big model.

01:16:22.811 --> 01:16:24.860
We label a few samples

01:16:24.861 --> 01:16:26.940
from the data with it
and we use it to train

01:16:26.941 --> 01:16:29.340
a much smaller specialized model

01:16:29.341 --> 01:16:32.500
and this model is
specialized to my query.

01:16:32.501 --> 01:16:35.005
It doesn't need to
recognize a pan

01:16:35.006 --> 01:16:37.820
dies or motorcycles
or things like that,

01:16:37.821 --> 01:16:39.525
it just needs to recognize cars.

01:16:39.526 --> 01:16:42.020
It's also specialized to
my data distribution.

01:16:42.021 --> 01:16:43.480
For example, maybe my camera

01:16:43.481 --> 01:16:44.790
is looking down at the street,

01:16:44.791 --> 01:16:46.560
so I don't need to worry about

01:16:46.561 --> 01:16:49.050
cars that are flipped upside
down and things like that.

01:16:49.051 --> 01:16:50.680
So, these are reasons why from

01:16:50.681 --> 01:16:52.200
a machine learning
standpoint you can

01:16:52.201 --> 01:16:55.500
have a smaller capacity
model that is pretty

01:16:55.501 --> 01:16:58.325
accurate for this
particular query and

01:16:58.326 --> 01:17:00.750
the other cool thing
about this model is we

01:17:00.751 --> 01:17:03.330
can train it to also
output a confidence score.

01:17:03.331 --> 01:17:06.180
So, when it's not sure
about the label of a frame,

01:17:06.181 --> 01:17:08.695
we can always call
the original model.

01:17:08.696 --> 01:17:11.680
It turns out that by tuning
basically the size of

01:17:11.681 --> 01:17:14.410
this model and
the confidence thresholds,

01:17:14.411 --> 01:17:16.000
you can actually capture most

01:17:16.001 --> 01:17:17.810
of the accuracy of the big model

01:17:17.811 --> 01:17:21.655
and still save 99% of
the invocations to it.

01:17:21.656 --> 01:17:26.100
So, we also design of
a cost-based optimizer that can

01:17:26.101 --> 01:17:28.510
solve this problem
of how exactly which

01:17:28.511 --> 01:17:31.335
specialized model to use
and which thresholds.

01:17:31.336 --> 01:17:34.105
Basically, the results
from this are that,

01:17:34.106 --> 01:17:36.515
depending on
your accuracy target,

01:17:36.516 --> 01:17:40.410
you can get large speedups
often an order of magnitude or

01:17:40.411 --> 01:17:44.070
more over these original models

01:17:44.071 --> 01:17:46.150
and still capture
a lot of the accuracy.

01:17:46.151 --> 01:17:48.730
So, we evaluated seven streams

01:17:48.731 --> 01:17:51.020
and this first paper
we had on it and

01:17:51.021 --> 01:17:54.970
basically decides the best one

01:17:54.971 --> 01:17:57.490
where you could go
thousands of times

01:17:57.491 --> 01:18:00.270
faster and keep 95 percent
of the accuracy.

01:18:00.271 --> 01:18:01.850
Even in the worst sides
you can go about

01:18:01.851 --> 01:18:03.670
an order of magnitude faster.

01:18:03.671 --> 01:18:07.460
So, it's a pretty powerful
technique to use for inference

01:18:07.461 --> 01:18:12.255
and it's easy to apply
probably beyond video as well.

01:18:12.256 --> 01:18:15.070
Just as an example of extending

01:18:15.071 --> 01:18:17.875
this further and ongoing work
which is unarchive,

01:18:17.876 --> 01:18:19.270
we're also extending this to

01:18:19.271 --> 01:18:21.550
more complex SQL-like queries

01:18:21.551 --> 01:18:23.930
where we see a query
and we use a mix of

01:18:23.931 --> 01:18:26.350
techniques including
model specialization

01:18:26.351 --> 01:18:28.970
and appoximate
query processing to design

01:18:28.971 --> 01:18:31.380
a very fast inference
pipeline for

01:18:31.381 --> 01:18:34.829
that query that meets
a certain target accuracy.

01:18:34.830 --> 01:18:36.580
So, you can read
about that as well

01:18:36.581 --> 01:18:39.120
to see how you can combine
these different things.

01:18:39.121 --> 01:18:41.000
It does a little bit

01:18:41.001 --> 01:18:43.440
more than just model
specialization and that.

01:18:43.441 --> 01:18:46.330
So, that's an example on
the inference side of

01:18:46.331 --> 01:18:48.080
the problems that are needed to

01:18:48.081 --> 01:18:51.520
actually use these
models in production.

01:18:51.521 --> 01:18:55.750
The next step I'll talk about
and the final Dawn example,

01:18:55.751 --> 01:18:58.705
I'll talk about is for
software development and

01:18:58.706 --> 01:19:01.890
the approach we have
several projects looking at

01:19:01.891 --> 01:19:05.455
here is designing
end-to-end compiler that let

01:19:05.456 --> 01:19:09.160
productivity programmers get
excellent performance and

01:19:09.161 --> 01:19:12.445
have basically built
a production grade application.

01:19:12.446 --> 01:19:14.090
So, I'll talk about,

01:19:14.091 --> 01:19:16.080
specifically one of my projects

01:19:16.081 --> 01:19:17.445
here which is called Weld.

01:19:17.446 --> 01:19:19.365
So, if you look at

01:19:19.366 --> 01:19:22.275
the data science machine
learning space,

01:19:22.276 --> 01:19:23.910
there are a ton of different

01:19:23.911 --> 01:19:25.490
processing steps you want to do

01:19:25.491 --> 01:19:28.780
with data and there's
a huge ecosystem of libraries,

01:19:28.781 --> 01:19:32.250
and also you want to
experiment really quickly.

01:19:32.251 --> 01:19:35.170
So, programmer productivity
is super important and

01:19:35.171 --> 01:19:37.380
the main technique we
have for a program

01:19:37.381 --> 01:19:39.725
or productivity in
general is composition.

01:19:39.726 --> 01:19:41.460
It's the only way that we've made

01:19:41.461 --> 01:19:43.580
it more efficient to
develop software.

01:19:43.581 --> 01:19:45.490
So, ML app developers will

01:19:45.491 --> 01:19:48.565
compose functions from
all these high-level libraries,

01:19:48.566 --> 01:19:50.820
thousands of Python packages,

01:19:50.821 --> 01:19:53.530
R packages, Spark
packages, et cetera.

01:19:53.531 --> 01:19:56.280
So, for example, you
might use something like

01:19:56.281 --> 01:19:58.970
Pandas in Python to
clean up some data,

01:19:58.971 --> 01:20:00.930
you might use NumPy to do

01:20:00.931 --> 01:20:02.980
some operations on
it, to normalize it.

01:20:02.981 --> 01:20:05.130
Then, you might use
a machine learning library like

01:20:05.131 --> 01:20:08.530
Scikit-learn and so you'll be
composing these functions.

01:20:08.531 --> 01:20:11.085
Now, the interesting thing
about this is that it

01:20:11.086 --> 01:20:15.070
creates an important problem

01:20:15.071 --> 01:20:17.655
for optimization
which is even though

01:20:17.656 --> 01:20:19.630
each individual function
you call might

01:20:19.631 --> 01:20:21.980
be highly optimized
and they often are,

01:20:21.981 --> 01:20:25.685
these numerical routines
are very carefully tuned,

01:20:25.686 --> 01:20:28.120
your end-to-end pipeline
could be extremely

01:20:28.121 --> 01:20:31.590
inefficient, and that's bad.

01:20:31.591 --> 01:20:33.330
It means that you're
training will be

01:20:33.331 --> 01:20:35.845
slow and then your production
serving will be slow.

01:20:35.846 --> 01:20:38.270
In many companies actually
the data scientists

01:20:38.271 --> 01:20:40.680
have to throw their
code over a fence to

01:20:40.681 --> 01:20:42.730
a production software
engineering team that

01:20:42.731 --> 01:20:45.010
rewrites it to make it
efficient and that's

01:20:45.011 --> 01:20:46.740
obviously not going to

01:20:46.741 --> 01:20:50.150
be cheap or and it's not
something we want to do.

01:20:50.151 --> 01:20:55.100
So, the one reason this
happens is just because

01:20:55.101 --> 01:20:57.510
the traditional interface
for composing

01:20:57.511 --> 01:20:59.170
these libraries
doesn't give you a lot

01:20:59.171 --> 01:21:01.375
of room to optimize.

01:21:01.376 --> 01:21:03.760
In particular,
most libraries have

01:21:03.761 --> 01:21:06.080
these interfaces written in
terms of function calls,

01:21:06.081 --> 01:21:08.660
that's the abstraction in
every programming language

01:21:08.661 --> 01:21:13.050
and to write a library
that operates on big data,

01:21:13.051 --> 01:21:14.670
you give your function pointer to

01:21:14.671 --> 01:21:16.510
data that's sitting
somewhere in memory.

01:21:16.511 --> 01:21:18.800
That's usually how you
pass things still.

01:21:18.801 --> 01:21:20.465
So, this is an example of

01:21:20.466 --> 01:21:23.175
a bunch of functions
I'm calling in Python.

01:21:23.176 --> 01:21:26.355
I'm going to parse and
then filter some data

01:21:26.356 --> 01:21:27.920
and then compute an average

01:21:27.921 --> 01:21:29.690
and then this is what
actually happened.

01:21:29.691 --> 01:21:32.190
So, these functions in Pandas and

01:21:32.191 --> 01:21:33.650
NumPy are actually all hated

01:21:33.651 --> 01:21:35.430
and see they're well optimized.

01:21:35.431 --> 01:21:37.100
But, what's really
going to happen is

01:21:37.101 --> 01:21:38.800
I'm going to call
each one separately and

01:21:38.801 --> 01:21:41.140
scan through the
data to read it out

01:21:41.141 --> 01:21:42.280
of memory and then write it

01:21:42.281 --> 01:21:43.795
back each time I go through it.

01:21:43.796 --> 01:21:45.770
So, for example, parse CSV,

01:21:45.771 --> 01:21:47.750
the interface is I
give you a pointer to

01:21:47.751 --> 01:21:49.760
the input string and I give you

01:21:49.761 --> 01:21:51.900
a pointer to an output buffer
and you parse it.

01:21:51.901 --> 01:21:53.035
So, has to do

01:21:53.036 --> 01:21:55.810
basically two scans through
the data, then dropna.

01:21:55.811 --> 01:21:58.245
A again, the interfaces
I gave you an array,

01:21:58.246 --> 01:21:59.535
you gave me a new array,

01:21:59.536 --> 01:22:03.080
it's doing two to the data
again and finally in the mean,

01:22:03.081 --> 01:22:04.825
I'm going to do a single array.

01:22:04.826 --> 01:22:07.080
Of course, memory access time

01:22:07.081 --> 01:22:08.850
dominates especially
for big data.

01:22:08.851 --> 01:22:12.210
So, in a real application,

01:22:12.211 --> 01:22:15.500
this can lead to a significant
slowdown and we actually

01:22:15.501 --> 01:22:17.880
measure it and just sort
of example workloads

01:22:17.881 --> 01:22:19.495
and popular frameworks,

01:22:19.496 --> 01:22:21.920
even though each
operator is optimize,

01:22:21.921 --> 01:22:24.210
they use dozens of
operators and you get

01:22:24.211 --> 01:22:25.610
these very large overheads from

01:22:25.611 --> 01:22:27.580
just data movement across them.

01:22:27.581 --> 01:22:30.510
So, in the Weld
project at Stanford,

01:22:30.511 --> 01:22:32.700
what we're doing is we're
basically designing

01:22:32.701 --> 01:22:35.850
a common intermediate
representation and

01:22:35.851 --> 01:22:37.475
a common run-time for

01:22:37.476 --> 01:22:40.020
data analytics libraries similar

01:22:40.021 --> 01:22:42.680
to like a language
virtual machine,

01:22:42.681 --> 01:22:44.925
but designed for
data parallel code,

01:22:44.926 --> 01:22:46.840
and the idea is these libraries

01:22:46.841 --> 01:22:48.230
submits the computation they

01:22:48.231 --> 01:22:51.920
want to do to design time engine,

01:22:51.921 --> 01:22:54.570
it leisurely collects
the computation from

01:22:54.571 --> 01:22:56.060
different libraries
and then it can

01:22:56.061 --> 01:22:58.290
optimize it for
different backends,

01:22:58.291 --> 01:23:01.220
and this can lead to
significant speed-ups as well.

01:23:01.221 --> 01:23:04.925
So, this is an example of
a Pandas and NumPy pipeline,

01:23:04.926 --> 01:23:08.200
it's combining operator
is that all written in C,

01:23:08.201 --> 01:23:10.805
but if you turn on Weld,

01:23:10.806 --> 01:23:13.890
you got around authentic
speedup on one-third.

01:23:13.891 --> 01:23:16.900
If you turn on class library
optimization and Weld,

01:23:16.901 --> 01:23:18.590
you get another factor of C,

01:23:18.591 --> 01:23:21.530
and then Weld's intermediate
language is actually,

01:23:21.531 --> 01:23:24.125
it's a functional language
that's data-parallel.

01:23:24.126 --> 01:23:27.220
So, it's trivial to
safely parallelize it.

01:23:27.221 --> 01:23:28.950
So, you can also turn on

01:23:28.951 --> 01:23:31.700
multi-threading underneath
these existing libraries

01:23:31.701 --> 01:23:33.045
and get a large speedup.

01:23:33.046 --> 01:23:36.400
We have a few papers on the
system and also on how we

01:23:36.401 --> 01:23:40.030
optimize underneath it
for real workloads.

01:23:40.031 --> 01:23:42.350
Then again, so just

01:23:42.351 --> 01:23:44.410
to give you a sense
of some ongoing work,

01:23:44.411 --> 01:23:46.800
this shows that okay optimizing

01:23:46.801 --> 01:23:49.765
across libraries is
useful and it gives,

01:23:49.766 --> 01:23:51.480
we tried to make
this as lightweight

01:23:51.481 --> 01:23:53.250
as possible to give users

01:23:53.251 --> 01:23:55.030
a really clean and simple

01:23:55.031 --> 01:23:57.060
intermediate language
they can write stuff in,

01:23:57.061 --> 01:23:58.980
but it's a bunch of
work for them to

01:23:58.981 --> 01:24:01.730
instrument and modify
all these libraries to use it.

01:24:01.731 --> 01:24:04.455
So, actually in this ongoing work

01:24:04.456 --> 01:24:06.680
where we're doing
Weld without world,

01:24:06.681 --> 01:24:10.020
we have abstraction called
split-ability annotations,

01:24:10.021 --> 01:24:13.380
and this is a way to enable
most of the optimizations

01:24:13.381 --> 01:24:16.610
that Weld gives you an
unmodified black-box functions.

01:24:16.611 --> 01:24:18.905
We don't even need the source
code of your function.

01:24:18.906 --> 01:24:22.465
So, for example in
MKL, the Intel mass,

01:24:22.466 --> 01:24:24.600
coronal library, all these

01:24:24.601 --> 01:24:27.010
hand optimized routines
for linear algebra.

01:24:27.011 --> 01:24:28.410
One of them is actually

01:24:28.411 --> 01:24:30.370
adding a bunch of
vectors and they're

01:24:30.371 --> 01:24:32.080
really fast and if
my program just

01:24:32.081 --> 01:24:34.390
needed to add two vectors,
this would be awesome.

01:24:34.391 --> 01:24:35.960
But my program
actually needs to do

01:24:35.961 --> 01:24:38.280
hundreds of linear
algebra operations

01:24:38.281 --> 01:24:40.260
and these functions
are designed to

01:24:40.261 --> 01:24:43.260
take the whole thing at once.
So, it's not that good.

01:24:43.261 --> 01:24:46.550
So, what we can do and
this might be my program,

01:24:46.551 --> 01:24:48.435
it's adding lots of
vectors together.

01:24:48.436 --> 01:24:50.810
What we can do with
split-ability annotations

01:24:50.811 --> 01:24:53.090
as we add an annotation
to each function that

01:24:53.091 --> 01:24:55.300
says how we can split up

01:24:55.301 --> 01:24:58.800
the data to call the function
on little chunks of it.

01:24:58.801 --> 01:25:00.360
This is very similar to

01:25:00.361 --> 01:25:03.450
vectorized execution and in

01:25:03.451 --> 01:25:05.730
databases with little batches.

01:25:05.731 --> 01:25:07.570
Is just adding that annotation

01:25:07.571 --> 01:25:09.145
to say how we can split this up.

01:25:09.146 --> 01:25:11.090
In this case, it means these are

01:25:11.091 --> 01:25:13.380
arrays that we're
adding element-wise,

01:25:13.381 --> 01:25:15.480
so you can split them
anyway you want into

01:25:15.481 --> 01:25:16.730
little chunks as long as

01:25:16.731 --> 01:25:18.820
the chunks have
the same shape across them.

01:25:18.821 --> 01:25:20.240
So, we have this type system

01:25:20.241 --> 01:25:24.605
here that can express more
complicated ways of splitting,

01:25:24.606 --> 01:25:26.030
and once you do that,

01:25:26.031 --> 01:25:28.690
you can actually get the
significant speed-ups basically

01:25:28.691 --> 01:25:31.950
from pipelining underneath
these functions.

01:25:31.951 --> 01:25:33.510
So, in this case,

01:25:33.511 --> 01:25:36.750
even though MKL itself
is multi-threaded,

01:25:36.751 --> 01:25:38.555
it's well optimized and so on,

01:25:38.556 --> 01:25:43.450
we got in this case a factor
of eight speedup in various,

01:25:43.451 --> 01:25:45.910
this is actually in
the simple workload

01:25:45.911 --> 01:25:48.440
but also in a bunch
of real workloads by

01:25:48.441 --> 01:25:51.080
pipelining the execution
of them essentially and

01:25:51.081 --> 01:25:52.420
it's pretty cool
because we never had to

01:25:52.421 --> 01:25:54.665
change the code of MKL.

01:25:54.666 --> 01:25:57.305
So, this is still
work in progress but

01:25:57.306 --> 01:25:59.460
the interesting result
from this is it can

01:25:59.461 --> 01:26:01.730
often get competitive performance

01:26:01.731 --> 01:26:03.980
with systems like Weld or XLA,

01:26:03.981 --> 01:26:06.070
or other compiler based systems

01:26:06.071 --> 01:26:07.770
without rewriting these library.

01:26:07.771 --> 01:26:09.470
So, it's a super
lightweight way to

01:26:09.471 --> 01:26:11.520
add stuff to an existing library.

01:26:11.521 --> 01:26:13.120
So, that's an example

01:26:13.121 --> 01:26:16.250
of making programmers
more productive.

01:26:16.251 --> 01:26:19.240
Okay, and then the final
bit I'll talk about now

01:26:19.241 --> 01:26:22.675
putting on my industry
hat for a bit

01:26:22.676 --> 01:26:26.010
is this new type of system for

01:26:26.011 --> 01:26:28.110
machine learning guide
industrial scale

01:26:28.111 --> 01:26:29.730
which is a machine-learning
platforms,

01:26:29.731 --> 01:26:32.700
and what's really interesting
about this is basically,

01:26:32.701 --> 01:26:34.200
I think hundreds of companies

01:26:34.201 --> 01:26:35.820
are building these platforms.

01:26:35.821 --> 01:26:37.110
It's a real need that

01:26:37.111 --> 01:26:39.480
actually engineering teams
building these things,

01:26:39.481 --> 01:26:40.940
but there's not a lot of research

01:26:40.941 --> 01:26:42.130
about it and I think it's

01:26:42.131 --> 01:26:44.690
an important system
to know about if

01:26:44.691 --> 01:26:47.595
you want to look at
the research in this field,

01:26:47.596 --> 01:26:51.045
there's also a lot of unknowns
and ways to innovate here.

01:26:51.046 --> 01:26:54.020
So, basically the question
here is if you believe in

01:26:54.021 --> 01:26:56.945
machine learning will be
a key part of future products,

01:26:56.946 --> 01:26:59.570
what should be the software
development process for it.

01:26:59.571 --> 01:27:02.360
So, we see this in a lot
of the companies that I

01:27:02.361 --> 01:27:03.630
talk with for example at

01:27:03.631 --> 01:27:05.530
Databricks as well
as at Stanford.

01:27:05.531 --> 01:27:06.760
They said okay, we had

01:27:06.761 --> 01:27:09.210
one or two projects that
were successful with

01:27:09.211 --> 01:27:11.710
machine learning but
they took a lot of

01:27:11.711 --> 01:27:14.950
time and they were
expensive to do.

01:27:14.951 --> 01:27:16.980
How can I have hundreds of teams

01:27:16.981 --> 01:27:19.050
in my company using
machine learning?

01:27:19.051 --> 01:27:23.450
How can I make it not
just a one-off like

01:27:23.451 --> 01:27:25.490
sort of experiments but

01:27:25.491 --> 01:27:28.260
something that every team
can reliably use?

01:27:28.261 --> 01:27:31.920
And today unfortunately,
just machine learning out

01:27:31.921 --> 01:27:35.570
of the box is the development
for it is very ad-hoc.

01:27:35.571 --> 01:27:39.565
So, there are a few problems
that make this difficult.

01:27:39.566 --> 01:27:42.940
So, one problem is just
tracking experiments.

01:27:42.941 --> 01:27:44.760
So, when you do machine learning,

01:27:44.761 --> 01:27:47.340
you're going to be running
many variations of your code.

01:27:47.341 --> 01:27:48.880
I'm sure in the
previous two talks,

01:27:48.881 --> 01:27:50.900
there were many versions
of the models that

01:27:50.901 --> 01:27:52.150
didn't work and then finally

01:27:52.151 --> 01:27:53.710
use are the ones that did work,

01:27:53.711 --> 01:27:56.780
and today, there's
no first class support for

01:27:56.781 --> 01:27:57.920
this in the systems you're doing

01:27:57.921 --> 01:27:59.885
software development in general.

01:27:59.886 --> 01:28:02.470
You have a linear set of gametes.

01:28:02.471 --> 01:28:04.910
You never have like
hundreds of branches that

01:28:04.911 --> 01:28:08.340
you need to track and
compare with each other.

01:28:08.341 --> 01:28:10.190
So, every data scientist has

01:28:10.191 --> 01:28:12.110
their own way of
managing experiments.

01:28:12.111 --> 01:28:13.300
It's really important to

01:28:13.301 --> 01:28:15.280
reproduce the results
and it's also very

01:28:15.281 --> 01:28:18.700
difficult because you have
dependencies on datasets,

01:28:18.701 --> 01:28:20.560
on code, on parameters,

01:28:20.561 --> 01:28:21.970
all over the place and again,

01:28:21.971 --> 01:28:24.680
there is no automatic way to
capture them and it's also

01:28:24.681 --> 01:28:26.110
difficult to share and manage

01:28:26.111 --> 01:28:27.600
models actually for
some companies,

01:28:27.601 --> 01:28:29.580
just tracking
which model is deployed,

01:28:29.581 --> 01:28:31.600
where did it come from is

01:28:31.601 --> 01:28:34.230
a non-trivial problem because

01:28:34.231 --> 01:28:36.440
there could be
thousands of models.

01:28:36.441 --> 01:28:38.420
So, we need sort
of the equivalent

01:28:38.421 --> 01:28:40.090
of software development
platforms if you

01:28:40.091 --> 01:28:41.310
think of something like building

01:28:41.311 --> 01:28:43.800
a web application or
a mobile application,

01:28:43.801 --> 01:28:46.420
there's so much infrastructure
around testing,

01:28:46.421 --> 01:28:48.695
IDEs, load balancers,

01:28:48.696 --> 01:28:51.450
quality assurance that it's
just there out of the box.

01:28:51.451 --> 01:28:53.950
You don't need to worry
about it, but again,

01:28:53.951 --> 01:28:55.770
machine learning is
different because of

01:28:55.771 --> 01:28:58.900
the wide range of
parameters and experiments.

01:28:58.901 --> 01:29:01.040
So, many companies are actually

01:29:01.041 --> 01:29:02.790
building a new class
of system to do it

01:29:02.791 --> 01:29:04.020
that I'm just going to name

01:29:04.021 --> 01:29:06.440
ML platforms because that's
why they named them.

01:29:06.441 --> 01:29:09.380
Some of the more prominent
examples that people

01:29:09.381 --> 01:29:12.650
wrote blog posts about are
the large web companies,

01:29:12.651 --> 01:29:16.750
so Facebook, FBlearner, Uber
Michelangelo Google TFX,

01:29:16.751 --> 01:29:20.440
but there are also many of
these at smaller companies and

01:29:20.441 --> 01:29:21.860
also even when I talk to

01:29:21.861 --> 01:29:25.000
individual machine learning
grad students or researchers,

01:29:25.001 --> 01:29:26.140
they say, "Oh, yeah, I have

01:29:26.141 --> 01:29:27.590
a Python library that

01:29:27.591 --> 01:29:29.730
tries to record
experiments for me."

01:29:29.731 --> 01:29:33.180
That's what they're doing.
So, these platforms,

01:29:33.181 --> 01:29:34.690
the way these usually
work is there's

01:29:34.691 --> 01:29:36.380
an engineering team
that says, okay,

01:29:36.381 --> 01:29:38.930
here are the 10
algorithms that you can

01:29:38.931 --> 01:29:42.115
use for machine learning and
if you use one of these 10,

01:29:42.116 --> 01:29:43.720
we will handle deployment,

01:29:43.721 --> 01:29:46.130
we'll handle learning experiments

01:29:46.131 --> 01:29:47.755
tuning parameters and so on,

01:29:47.756 --> 01:29:49.680
so we'll handle
the whole life cycle.

01:29:49.681 --> 01:29:51.570
So, it's good because
they standardized

01:29:51.571 --> 01:29:52.920
the life cycle you know that if

01:29:52.921 --> 01:29:54.400
you work within this platform,

01:29:54.401 --> 01:29:56.250
it will remember
what you did and let

01:29:56.251 --> 01:29:58.590
you deploy it and let
your whole backend stuff.

01:29:58.591 --> 01:30:00.800
But just having
everyone build their

01:30:00.801 --> 01:30:02.990
own is also disadvantageous.

01:30:02.991 --> 01:30:04.720
First of all, they're
each limited to

01:30:04.721 --> 01:30:06.330
whatever frameworks or algorithms

01:30:06.331 --> 01:30:08.190
that company decided to support,

01:30:08.191 --> 01:30:10.120
and engineers have
to add a new one,

01:30:10.121 --> 01:30:11.730
and second, they're also tied

01:30:11.731 --> 01:30:13.190
to this internal infrastructure.

01:30:13.191 --> 01:30:14.790
So, you can't do really and share

01:30:14.791 --> 01:30:17.485
these things across companies.

01:30:17.486 --> 01:30:18.970
So, one of the projects

01:30:18.971 --> 01:30:20.930
that I'm working
on at Databricks,

01:30:20.931 --> 01:30:22.690
is actually an Open Source,

01:30:22.691 --> 01:30:25.270
Open-Interface machine learning
platform that we

01:30:25.271 --> 01:30:28.080
launched back in June this year.

01:30:28.081 --> 01:30:29.400
It's called MLflow.

01:30:29.401 --> 01:30:31.480
And I'll just give you
a sense of what it can do,

01:30:31.481 --> 01:30:34.560
but you can read about this
and the other ones online,

01:30:34.561 --> 01:30:36.690
just to give you a sense
of the problems.

01:30:36.691 --> 01:30:40.380
So, one of the things that it
does is, by Open-Interface,

01:30:40.381 --> 01:30:43.290
I mean that it's designed to

01:30:43.291 --> 01:30:45.240
work with whatever infrastructure

01:30:45.241 --> 01:30:46.710
you have and whatever algorithm,

01:30:46.711 --> 01:30:47.810
as opposed to like, "here are

01:30:47.811 --> 01:30:49.515
ten algorithms that we support."

01:30:49.516 --> 01:30:51.710
So, one of the things it does is,

01:30:51.711 --> 01:30:55.150
it lets you package
reproducible projects.

01:30:55.151 --> 01:31:03.190
So basically, you can
declare what dependencies,

01:31:03.191 --> 01:31:05.570
what environment
your machine learning code

01:31:05.571 --> 01:31:06.895
needs in order to run,

01:31:06.896 --> 01:31:08.970
and you can also
declare parameters that

01:31:08.971 --> 01:31:11.160
other people from outside
can use to call it,

01:31:11.161 --> 01:31:13.475
without having to
know the details.

01:31:13.476 --> 01:31:14.880
And then in your code,

01:31:14.881 --> 01:31:18.410
you use these parameters
and you do your work.

01:31:18.411 --> 01:31:20.520
So, that means you can
package your project and

01:31:20.521 --> 01:31:22.920
run it again and get
the same results later.

01:31:22.921 --> 01:31:27.485
Second component it has,
is experiment tracking.

01:31:27.486 --> 01:31:29.340
In your code, you basically use

01:31:29.341 --> 01:31:31.510
this REST API to log information,

01:31:31.511 --> 01:31:33.070
like here are my parameters,

01:31:33.071 --> 01:31:34.200
here are my metrics,

01:31:34.201 --> 01:31:35.540
here is the model I built.

01:31:35.541 --> 01:31:37.970
There's also a way of
representing a model.

01:31:37.971 --> 01:31:41.410
And then, MLflow basically
builds a giant database of

01:31:41.411 --> 01:31:42.960
all the experiments
that have been

01:31:42.961 --> 01:31:44.690
on for a specific goal,

01:31:44.691 --> 01:31:47.195
and you can go back and
re-run a specific one,

01:31:47.196 --> 01:31:48.820
or compare them, or pull out

01:31:48.821 --> 01:31:50.690
the models and do
something with them.

01:31:50.691 --> 01:31:52.470
And then, the third part
in this handle

01:31:52.471 --> 01:31:55.010
is deployment, which is,

01:31:55.011 --> 01:31:56.700
once I've got a model,

01:31:56.701 --> 01:32:00.500
it actually represents models
as basically a function,

01:32:00.501 --> 01:32:02.840
very similar to
a serverless function.

01:32:02.841 --> 01:32:05.780
And we can then deploy the same
model to many different

01:32:05.781 --> 01:32:07.110
'Inference Tools' such as

01:32:07.111 --> 01:32:09.890
"Batch" and "Streaming
Inference."

01:32:10.930 --> 01:32:15.340
Basically, it's a pretty simple
kind of workflow system,

01:32:15.341 --> 01:32:17.730
but it's also something
that has a bunch of

01:32:17.731 --> 01:32:20.240
interesting data management
and systems challenges.

01:32:20.241 --> 01:32:23.910
How exactly should I represent
projects and dependencies?

01:32:23.911 --> 01:32:25.275
How should I represent

01:32:25.276 --> 01:32:27.030
the experiments? How
should I equate?

01:32:27.031 --> 01:32:28.865
I mean, I can imagine
queries like,

01:32:28.866 --> 01:32:31.315
"I had a thousand different
versions of a model,

01:32:31.316 --> 01:32:32.600
and I have a new data point,

01:32:32.601 --> 01:32:34.490
and I want to know which one
does best on them."

01:32:34.491 --> 01:32:36.410
There are a lot of
interesting problems

01:32:36.411 --> 01:32:38.450
to look at within this space.

01:32:38.451 --> 01:32:41.495
And if you want to read about
this or the other ones,

01:32:41.496 --> 01:32:43.810
I think it's an interesting
thing to look at,

01:32:43.811 --> 01:32:46.590
because again, people are
actually building these,

01:32:46.591 --> 01:32:48.170
like hundreds of companies have

01:32:48.171 --> 01:32:51.330
independently decided that
they need to build a platform,

01:32:51.331 --> 01:32:52.695
but I don't really see,

01:32:52.696 --> 01:32:55.325
when I look at a database class
or something like that,

01:32:55.326 --> 01:32:56.670
I don't actually see anyone

01:32:56.671 --> 01:32:58.825
talking about
this class of system.

01:32:58.826 --> 01:33:01.075
And there's probably
a lot that can be done

01:33:01.076 --> 01:33:03.670
to make these things better.

01:33:03.671 --> 01:33:07.175
Many open questions left in
designing these platforms.

01:33:07.176 --> 01:33:09.100
Okay, so that was kind of

01:33:09.101 --> 01:33:12.605
my brief foray into what's
happening in industry.

01:33:12.606 --> 01:33:15.610
To conclude, the limiting factors

01:33:15.611 --> 01:33:17.235
of machine learning adoption,

01:33:17.236 --> 01:33:19.620
are the development
and productionization

01:33:19.621 --> 01:33:22.000
tools for many users,
many real users,

01:33:22.001 --> 01:33:23.570
especially the ones with

01:33:23.571 --> 01:33:26.990
really high impact
business-use cases,

01:33:26.991 --> 01:33:28.905
and they're not
the training algorithms.

01:33:28.906 --> 01:33:29.630
I think this is

01:33:29.631 --> 01:33:31.640
a really great opportunity
for researchers,

01:33:31.641 --> 01:33:33.460
including data
management researchers,

01:33:33.461 --> 01:33:35.840
because a lot of it
is a data problem,

01:33:35.841 --> 01:33:37.840
and they're very unexplored.

01:33:37.841 --> 01:33:39.470
You can follow the research we're

01:33:39.471 --> 01:33:41.570
doing on DAWN in our blog,

01:33:41.571 --> 01:33:43.985
and I'm also happy to
chat with anyone about

01:33:43.986 --> 01:33:47.730
problems in this space. Thanks.

01:33:52.180 --> 01:33:55.865
&gt;&gt; Of course, we
are running late,

01:33:55.866 --> 01:34:00.820
but let's ask if you have
any questions for Matei,

01:34:00.821 --> 01:34:03.880
let's take a few.

01:34:03.881 --> 01:34:08.100
&gt;&gt; Hey. Thanks for the talk.
I love everything.

01:34:09.280 --> 01:34:10.550
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:34:10.551 --> 01:34:12.230
&gt;&gt; I have lots of
questions, but in

01:34:12.231 --> 01:34:14.310
the ML platform side
of things, right,

01:34:14.311 --> 01:34:17.005
I think especially as
we start applying this

01:34:17.006 --> 01:34:20.945
to optimize systems becomes
particularly problematic,

01:34:20.946 --> 01:34:22.755
because all of a
sudden, SQL Server

01:34:22.756 --> 01:34:23.870
stopped running correctly.

01:34:23.871 --> 01:34:24.650
&gt;&gt; All right, yes.

01:34:24.651 --> 01:34:26.180
&gt;&gt; Because something
somewhere happened.

01:34:26.181 --> 01:34:29.400
And now, I think that beyond
what you're describing,

01:34:29.401 --> 01:34:30.760
there seems to be missing sort

01:34:30.761 --> 01:34:33.650
of the equivalent of
software engineering practice.

01:34:33.651 --> 01:34:33.890
&gt;&gt; Right, yes.

01:34:33.891 --> 01:34:35.960
&gt;&gt; In software engineering,
we have like "Find

01:34:35.961 --> 01:34:38.140
Bugs" and you know
"Code Reviews" and

01:34:38.141 --> 01:34:40.490
all sorts of practices that
kind of protect us from

01:34:40.491 --> 01:34:42.220
the worst mistake and socialize

01:34:42.221 --> 01:34:44.830
our understanding of what the
system is supposed to do.

01:34:44.831 --> 01:34:48.380
Do you have any ideas, beside
tracking the experiments,

01:34:48.381 --> 01:34:49.600
like how we get into.

01:34:49.601 --> 01:34:50.680
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

01:34:50.681 --> 01:34:52.430
Yes, that's an awesome question.

01:34:52.431 --> 01:34:55.060
And yes, I think it
is missing and people

01:34:55.061 --> 01:34:57.780
are trying to come up
with best practices,

01:34:57.781 --> 01:34:59.530
so just as an example.

01:34:59.531 --> 01:35:01.380
So some of the things we want to

01:35:01.381 --> 01:35:03.300
enable with MLflow
is to tell people,

01:35:03.301 --> 01:35:05.205
"Hey, if you follow this process,

01:35:05.206 --> 01:35:06.790
write your code in a project,

01:35:06.791 --> 01:35:08.265
submit it this way,

01:35:08.266 --> 01:35:09.730
pull out the model this way,

01:35:09.731 --> 01:35:11.980
you will get the
following benefits."

01:35:11.981 --> 01:35:14.390
Here are some examples
I think I've seen.

01:35:14.391 --> 01:35:17.330
One thing I've seen
at one large company,

01:35:17.331 --> 01:35:20.655
is they actually have
leader-boards for common tasks.

01:35:20.656 --> 01:35:22.190
Let's say you're building

01:35:22.191 --> 01:35:25.735
a better language model or
a better image classifier,

01:35:25.736 --> 01:35:28.150
there's a single leader-board
in the organization,

01:35:28.151 --> 01:35:31.520
where you can see how
others have done over time,

01:35:31.521 --> 01:35:33.750
you can submit yours
and you can compare it.

01:35:33.751 --> 01:35:37.855
And that's a way that many
people can work on a problem,

01:35:37.856 --> 01:35:39.555
and you can see how it's doing.

01:35:39.556 --> 01:35:42.770
There's a lot of
interesting stuff with testing.

01:35:42.771 --> 01:35:44.915
Is my model doing worse than,

01:35:44.916 --> 01:35:46.575
say, a naive linear model?

01:35:46.576 --> 01:35:48.500
Is my data drifting in some way?

01:35:48.501 --> 01:35:49.970
Are my predictions drifting?

01:35:49.971 --> 01:35:51.730
Is the distribution
of the features

01:35:51.731 --> 01:35:53.570
I see in production different?

01:35:53.571 --> 01:35:56.640
For example, a common thing
is actually you have a bug,

01:35:56.641 --> 01:35:59.390
where you didn't load one of
your features into training,

01:35:59.391 --> 01:36:01.540
or it didn't get
transformed the same way.

01:36:01.541 --> 01:36:03.160
That's another practice.

01:36:03.161 --> 01:36:06.290
The other one that is
actually hard to talk about,

01:36:06.291 --> 01:36:08.950
but is actually even
defining the metric you

01:36:08.951 --> 01:36:12.040
want to change and making
it easy to swap metrics.

01:36:12.041 --> 01:36:14.535
One of the things that we
want to do in MLflow is,

01:36:14.536 --> 01:36:16.820
if you trained a bunch of models

01:36:16.821 --> 01:36:19.620
already and now you want to
change the evaluation metric,

01:36:19.621 --> 01:36:22.350
how can we easily let you
switch that and basically add

01:36:22.351 --> 01:36:24.010
a column to that table
and compare

01:36:24.011 --> 01:36:26.445
them on the new metric. Yes.

01:36:26.446 --> 01:36:29.060
&gt;&gt; Other questions?

01:36:29.940 --> 01:36:33.410
I think we don't

01:36:33.411 --> 01:36:35.480
have the time that we
have set for the panel,

01:36:35.481 --> 01:36:37.865
but this is related
to Carlos' question,

01:36:37.866 --> 01:36:39.735
and I would ask both you,

01:36:39.736 --> 01:36:41.680
Andy and Tim to comment

01:36:41.681 --> 01:36:43.930
on it if you have
any thoughts on it.

01:36:43.931 --> 01:36:46.450
This is really the software
engineering question,

01:36:46.451 --> 01:36:49.340
but sometimes for example,

01:36:49.341 --> 01:36:54.495
the kind of work Andy
and Tim are pursuing.

01:36:54.496 --> 01:36:56.915
There's always this old-fashioned

01:36:56.916 --> 01:36:58.350
database or systems people,

01:36:58.351 --> 01:37:00.300
the fear that something

01:37:00.301 --> 01:37:02.935
will go wrong in
a robustness sense.

01:37:02.936 --> 01:37:06.030
That yes, it's an average case,

01:37:06.031 --> 01:37:07.170
it's great, but certainly,

01:37:07.171 --> 01:37:09.475
we are going to be
exposed to a variance.

01:37:09.476 --> 01:37:11.970
And this is possible
for any system,

01:37:11.971 --> 01:37:13.120
but typically in a distributed

01:37:13.121 --> 01:37:14.330
system or a database system,

01:37:14.331 --> 01:37:15.575
that are guard rails.

01:37:15.576 --> 01:37:17.635
That, you know that
you've tripped that fuse,

01:37:17.636 --> 01:37:19.265
and we basically say, "Okay,

01:37:19.266 --> 01:37:21.120
things are not going well."

01:37:21.121 --> 01:37:23.020
Of course, you don't want to
trip the fuse all the time

01:37:23.021 --> 01:37:25.595
and have some
discipline around it,

01:37:25.596 --> 01:37:30.020
but is this manageable in the
world that we are entering?

01:37:30.021 --> 01:37:32.160
And this is also true when we're

01:37:32.161 --> 01:37:35.370
building such MLflow and so on,

01:37:35.371 --> 01:37:38.374
is there any support we
get from the system,

01:37:38.375 --> 01:37:40.650
for understanding this or

01:37:40.651 --> 01:37:42.765
experimenting with it
at the last scale?

01:37:42.766 --> 01:37:45.630
This is the only question that
I'd like all three of you

01:37:45.631 --> 01:37:48.910
to comment on it briefly if
you have any thoughts, right.

01:37:48.911 --> 01:37:50.510
Matei, you can go first,

01:37:50.511 --> 01:37:51.530
you're just right
here at the podium.

01:37:51.531 --> 01:37:52.740
&gt;&gt; Yes. The question is how to

01:37:52.741 --> 01:37:54.480
have guardrails for
machine learning,

01:37:54.481 --> 01:37:56.310
or getting [inaudible] behavior.

01:37:56.311 --> 01:37:58.880
I think it's a good question,
and I think so far,

01:37:58.881 --> 01:38:02.970
I've just seen people do
application-specific stuff.

01:38:02.971 --> 01:38:05.510
But basically, I think

01:38:05.511 --> 01:38:07.420
the most important
things I've seen

01:38:07.421 --> 01:38:09.370
are having a fallback,

01:38:09.371 --> 01:38:15.390
that is, maybe it gives
you lower quality results,

01:38:15.391 --> 01:38:18.125
but it's robust and you can
predict stuff about it.

01:38:18.126 --> 01:38:19.920
And then also having some kind of

01:38:19.921 --> 01:38:22.920
monitoring that tells you if
things are going haywire.

01:38:22.921 --> 01:38:26.460
But the way you do that can
be different in each system.

01:38:26.461 --> 01:38:29.390
Yes, I don't think
there's a guarantee.

01:38:29.391 --> 01:38:31.250
&gt;&gt; I think I understand
the fact that we

01:38:31.251 --> 01:38:33.850
can go to a safer alternative.

01:38:33.851 --> 01:38:34.145
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:38:34.146 --> 01:38:36.270
&gt;&gt; But to detect when
we should do that,.

01:38:36.271 --> 01:38:36.800
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:38:36.801 --> 01:38:40.380
&gt;&gt; Is sometimes the most
challenging part,right.

01:38:40.381 --> 01:38:41.490
&gt;&gt; Yes, it can be.

01:38:41.491 --> 01:38:42.620
I think again partly,

01:38:42.621 --> 01:38:44.350
just data drift is

01:38:44.351 --> 01:38:46.240
the easiest of
these things to capture.

01:38:46.241 --> 01:38:48.010
Even that is not super easy.

01:38:48.011 --> 01:38:49.305
But like saying, "Hey,

01:38:49.306 --> 01:38:51.420
my predictions look
super different,

01:38:51.421 --> 01:38:54.255
or my input data look
super different." Yes.

01:38:54.256 --> 01:38:56.080
&gt;&gt; And I think it's even harder.

01:38:56.081 --> 01:38:57.490
It's not just falling back,

01:38:57.491 --> 01:38:59.140
it's falling back gracefully,

01:38:59.141 --> 01:39:00.940
because if Machine
learning give us

01:39:00.941 --> 01:39:03.110
20x better than where
we were before,

01:39:03.111 --> 01:39:04.970
falling back to before
is unacceptable.

01:39:04.971 --> 01:39:05.990
&gt;&gt; Yes, definitely.

01:39:05.991 --> 01:39:07.730
&gt;&gt; We should smoothly fall back.

01:39:07.731 --> 01:39:09.300
&gt;&gt; Yes, just in case you

01:39:09.301 --> 01:39:11.705
didn't hear Carlo,
Carlos' point was,

01:39:11.706 --> 01:39:13.750
just having a very step function

01:39:13.751 --> 01:39:17.225
like alternative to fall back on,

01:39:17.226 --> 01:39:19.625
is probably not desirable
for the system to have

01:39:19.626 --> 01:39:23.320
some ways to do it gracefully
to the extent possible.

01:39:23.321 --> 01:39:26.460
Tim and Andy, if you have
thoughts, come up here.

01:39:26.461 --> 01:39:27.799
We'll enable the microphones,

01:39:27.800 --> 01:39:29.360
unless you have given it up.

01:39:29.361 --> 01:39:31.695
And that will allow
you to speak here.

01:39:31.696 --> 01:39:35.710
Are you guys still tied up?

01:39:35.711 --> 01:39:38.550
&gt;&gt; I don't know. Yes,
I'm still hooked up.

01:39:38.551 --> 01:39:41.575
&gt;&gt; Can you turn
these folks all both on.

01:39:41.576 --> 01:39:43.720
&gt;&gt; Sure. They're on.

01:39:43.721 --> 01:39:44.870
&gt;&gt; Okay, so
the only thing I would

01:39:44.871 --> 01:39:46.010
add from Matei, I
think he's right,

01:39:46.011 --> 01:39:47.650
is you have to have
a clear objective function to

01:39:47.651 --> 01:39:49.730
determine when some things
are going bad.

01:39:49.731 --> 01:39:51.990
But the only thing I
would add is that,

01:39:51.991 --> 01:39:54.390
exploitation versus exploration,

01:39:54.391 --> 01:39:57.675
I think having
the algorithms be mindful

01:39:57.676 --> 01:39:59.740
of what is expected of you

01:39:59.741 --> 01:40:02.075
at a given time of
day or week or month,

01:40:02.076 --> 01:40:04.315
that way, if it's at nighttime,

01:40:04.316 --> 01:40:06.280
when your demand is
low, you can be more

01:40:06.281 --> 01:40:08.260
aggressive about trying
different things.

01:40:08.261 --> 01:40:09.740
But during the day, when you have

01:40:09.741 --> 01:40:11.130
enough SLAs you have to meet

01:40:11.131 --> 01:40:12.815
for transactions or your queries,

01:40:12.816 --> 01:40:14.430
then you want to be a bit more

01:40:14.431 --> 01:40:16.835
conservative and not
try something crazy.

01:40:16.836 --> 01:40:20.910
Maybe just not do
any tuning at all, right.

01:40:20.950 --> 01:40:22.430
&gt;&gt; Tim?

01:40:22.431 --> 01:40:24.320
&gt;&gt; My perspective is
a little bit different,

01:40:24.321 --> 01:40:26.000
because we mainly
want to use machine

01:40:26.001 --> 01:40:28.360
learning for improving
the systems,

01:40:28.361 --> 01:40:30.090
which is a different domain
than if I want

01:40:30.091 --> 01:40:32.170
to build a model for
detecting images.

01:40:32.171 --> 01:40:33.610
And I think, for example,

01:40:33.611 --> 01:40:35.760
for the indexing case,
we particular,

01:40:35.761 --> 01:40:37.120
are now looking into
techniques that we

01:40:37.121 --> 01:40:39.420
can find bounds for the arrows,

01:40:39.421 --> 01:40:40.845
we can get them the best case.

01:40:40.846 --> 01:40:43.880
There's this whole field of
blog about smooth analysis,

01:40:43.881 --> 01:40:45.070
which allows you to get,

01:40:45.071 --> 01:40:47.680
for certain types of
models, guarantees.

01:40:47.681 --> 01:40:49.370
And then, you can say, "Okay,

01:40:49.371 --> 01:40:51.950
in the worst case, I get the
following behavior," right?

01:40:51.951 --> 01:40:54.565
And like, "My model also
grows like a B-tree."

01:40:54.566 --> 01:40:56.500
Like the B-tree gives
you a strong guarantee,

01:40:56.501 --> 01:40:57.640
because you know in

01:40:57.641 --> 01:40:59.340
the moment it goes
like out of balance,

01:40:59.341 --> 01:41:00.850
you re-balance
the whole thing to get

01:41:00.851 --> 01:41:02.940
the same lookup guarantee again.

01:41:02.941 --> 01:41:05.230
There are some machine learning
models where you can do

01:41:05.231 --> 01:41:08.985
something similar. There's hope.

01:41:08.986 --> 01:41:10.630
For the new net stuff,

01:41:10.631 --> 01:41:12.010
it's just like
old nets right now,

01:41:12.011 --> 01:41:14.095
and there's not much you can do.

01:41:14.096 --> 01:41:15.880
You need to restrict
the tuples modes.

01:41:15.881 --> 01:41:17.070
In some domains, you can do that,

01:41:17.071 --> 01:41:18.770
and in some others, you can't.

01:41:18.771 --> 01:41:23.760
&gt;&gt; Any thoughts, questions
from the audience?

01:41:24.750 --> 01:41:27.205
Well, thank you all.

01:41:27.206 --> 01:41:30.540
Thank you very much for
a very entertaining session.

